[:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-54df9ca0-9526-59ba-8d0c-624262e20b11" :answer-field/id #uuid "54df9ca0-9526-59ba-8d0c-624262e20b11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-06386582-1858-5b59-a352-479a8fbec1ee" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c8679008-38bf-5c0c-91af-6a3638018d35" "answer-a7595e68-63e0-55b4-9190-55fd9ad8ee38" "answer-06386582-1858-5b59-a352-479a8fbec1ee" "answer-e5e08e8d-ec38-59a8-94ea-46959cbfc389" "answer-ed27e2cf-d889-5413-8f07-fe2a732afd99"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8679008-38bf-5c0c-91af-6a3638018d35" :answer/id #uuid "c8679008-38bf-5c0c-91af-6a3638018d35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{k}{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7595e68-63e0-55b4-9190-55fd9ad8ee38" :answer/id #uuid "a7595e68-63e0-55b4-9190-55fd9ad8ee38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k+m" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06386582-1858-5b59-a352-479a8fbec1ee" :answer/id #uuid "06386582-1858-5b59-a352-479a8fbec1ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k-m" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5e08e8d-ec38-59a8-94ea-46959cbfc389" :answer/id #uuid "e5e08e8d-ec38-59a8-94ea-46959cbfc389" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "km" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed27e2cf-d889-5413-8f07-fe2a732afd99" :answer/id #uuid "ed27e2cf-d889-5413-8f07-fe2a732afd99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "m-k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-99f3a3dc-a29b-56b3-b6b2-eb00c09e4e97" :question/id #uuid "99f3a3dc-a29b-56b3-b6b2-eb00c09e4e97" :question/math-academy-id "q-24442" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)=p$ and $\\operatorname{log}⁡(y)=q$, then $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{y})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the quotient rule for common logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(b)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for common logarithms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{y}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(y) \\\\  & =p-q\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2842ede1-7316-5852-8316-724915e58cee"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2842ede1-7316-5852-8316-724915e58cee" :answer-field/id #uuid "2842ede1-7316-5852-8316-724915e58cee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-475b221e-4c3d-508a-8794-b0910728ee87" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3ab9f307-2949-509c-aa21-cb7f2f79269e" "answer-8fadf0d1-ce58-5388-bbca-029241c7ef65" "answer-5a7458a8-57c1-50ff-a7eb-323cf41e0146" "answer-475b221e-4c3d-508a-8794-b0910728ee87" "answer-d4e5be19-84e6-54b6-a352-1c0c4517f128"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ab9f307-2949-509c-aa21-cb7f2f79269e" :answer/id #uuid "3ab9f307-2949-509c-aa21-cb7f2f79269e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{p}{q}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8fadf0d1-ce58-5388-bbca-029241c7ef65" :answer/id #uuid "8fadf0d1-ce58-5388-bbca-029241c7ef65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{q}{p}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a7458a8-57c1-50ff-a7eb-323cf41e0146" :answer/id #uuid "5a7458a8-57c1-50ff-a7eb-323cf41e0146" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p+q" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-475b221e-4c3d-508a-8794-b0910728ee87" :answer/id #uuid "475b221e-4c3d-508a-8794-b0910728ee87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p-q" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4e5be19-84e6-54b6-a352-1c0c4517f128" :answer/id #uuid "d4e5be19-84e6-54b6-a352-1c0c4517f128" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "q-p" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111878 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to rewrite $\\log_3(4) - \\log_3(6)$ as a single logarithm. We can do this using the **quotient rule**. \n\nThe quotient rule is very similar to the product rule, except that this time we're dealing with the difference of two logarithms rather than the sum. \n\nThe quotient rule states that two logarithms which share a common base $\\color{blue}n$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\log_{\\color{blue}n} (a) - \\log_{\\color{blue}n} (b) = \\log_{\\color{blue}n}\\left(\\dfrac a b\\right) .\n$$\n\nSo, we can use this rule to rewrite $\\log_{\\color{blue}3}(4) - \\log_{\\color{blue}3}(6)$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(4)-{log}_{3}⁡(6) & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{4}{6}) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule works exactly the same for the natural and common logarithms." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110843 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092172 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-597abfab-9fa0-5884-9650-9d3a1ca7b418" "question-7afac99d-b74c-555b-b269-d08cbbf29447" "question-c08c7d82-b9b5-59ff-a4f6-2f1eac5d7e57" "question-bf7fd672-fd24-5897-b0f7-1572fe68c96d" "question-44457776-708f-510a-afa3-90718ee8b770"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186091991 :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\cos{2x} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ for $0\\leq x < 2\\pi.$ If the solutions $x_1, x_2, x_3$ and $x_4$ are written in ascending order,  what is the value of $x_2\\cdot x_3?$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{7\\pi }{8}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{8n\\pi }{8} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{7\\pi }{8}+\\frac{8n\\pi }{8} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(8n+1)\\pi }{8} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(8n+7)\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2,\\ldots,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0 \\leq x \\lt 2\\pi.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$ and $x=\\dfrac{7\\pi}{8}.$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\dfrac{9\\pi}{8}$ and $x=\\dfrac{15\\pi}{8}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0 \\leq x \\lt 2\\pi.$ So, the solutions $x_1, x_2, x_3$ and $x_4$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{8}, \\, \\dfrac{7\\pi}{8}, \\, \\dfrac{9\\pi}{8}, \\, \\dfrac{15\\pi}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nx_2\\cdot x_3 =  \\dfrac{7\\pi}{8} \\cdot  \\dfrac{9\\pi}{8} = \\dfrac{63\\pi^2}{64}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092173 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0663d30d-9045-55b3-9c5e-7d0dcf4eda51" "question-689ab27d-73f0-5878-911e-f4bfe8b4c90e" "question-cd740014-1051-58c1-bf02-d54c44856f2e" "question-468c5cf8-2c4e-5dd6-96eb-d94e2d3f4648" "question-9acc1f61-f727-5595-afa3-5b687cb64df5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186091993 :question/problem "Solve the equation $-2\\sqrt{3}\\cos\\left(2x+60^\\circ\\right) - 3 = 0$ for $0^\\circ\\leq x <           360^\\circ.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{60}^{∘})-3 & =0 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{60}^{∘}) & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{60}^{∘}) & =-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{60}^{∘}) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+{60}^{∘} & ={150}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & 2x+{60}^{∘} & ={210}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={150}^{∘}-{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 2x & ={210}^{∘}-{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 2x & ={150}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={75}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2,\\ldots$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0^\\circ \\leq x \\lt 360^\\circ.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=45^\\circ$ and $x=75^\\circ.$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=225^\\circ$  and $x=255^\\circ.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0^\\circ \\leq x \\lt 360^\\circ.$ So, the solutions $x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 = 45^\\circ,\\quad x_2 = 75^\\circ,\\quad x_3 =  225^\\circ,\\quad x_4= 255^\\circ .\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092174 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8d85e958-8094-54f0-80e0-53c48caaf572" "question-f8328338-c374-57d6-bab7-26f0397ea50b" "question-ef6f56bf-b6a0-53ab-9821-d8f13185e30a" "question-637ba677-860c-50d3-9633-f9db8d1adb0f" "question-af242b6e-a0fa-5102-becb-649e55619922"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186091997 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\cos\\left(2x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}$ for $-2\\pi <          x \\leq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 2x+\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{11\\pi }{12}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{12}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{11\\pi }{24}+n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{24}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{(24n-11)\\pi }{24} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(24n+5)\\pi }{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2,\\ldots$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-2\\pi <          x \\leq 0.$\n\n- Setting $n=-2$ gives $x=-\\dfrac{59\\pi}{24}$ and $x=-\\dfrac{43\\pi}{24}.$ We reject the solution $-\\dfrac{59\\pi}{24}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-\\dfrac{35\\pi}{24}$ and $x=-\\dfrac{19\\pi}{24}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=-\\dfrac{11\\pi}{24}$ and $x=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{24}.$ We reject the solution $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{24}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-2\\pi <          x \\leq 0.$   So, the solutions $x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 = -\\dfrac{43\\pi}{24}, \\quad x_2=-\\dfrac{35\\pi}{24}, \\quad x_3 = -\\dfrac{19\\pi}{24}, \\quad x_4 = -\\dfrac{11\\pi}{24}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-597abfab-9fa0-5884-9650-9d3a1ca7b418" :question/id #uuid "597abfab-9fa0-5884-9650-9d3a1ca7b418" :question/math-academy-id "q-121513" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are written in ascending order,  what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{2} & ={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & \\frac{3x}{2} & ={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & ={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 3x & ={480}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘} \\\\ x & =\\frac{{240}^{∘}}{3}+\\frac{n\\cdot {720}^{∘}}{3} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{{480}^{∘}}{3}+\\frac{n\\cdot {720}^{∘}}{3} \\\\ x & ={80}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={160}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={80}^{∘}$ and $x={160}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={320}^{∘}$ and $x={400}^{∘}.$ We reject the solution ${400}^{∘}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx={80}^{∘},\\,{160}^{∘},\\,{320}^{∘}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}={80}^{∘}$ and ${x}_{3}={320}^{∘},$ the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\text{ }+{x}_{3}$ is $80+320=400.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e903e461-23e0-5fcd-b90c-a7d9090e44fa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e903e461-23e0-5fcd-b90c-a7d9090e44fa" :answer-field/id #uuid "e903e461-23e0-5fcd-b90c-a7d9090e44fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-011df462-573f-5d1e-a6c9-530e8e4108d9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-32606e7b-b40b-50e3-a1d1-5d1910806be4" "answer-be134ae9-dcf1-5a55-b9fd-d851e56132fb" "answer-011df462-573f-5d1e-a6c9-530e8e4108d9" "answer-90057f88-a3e0-5ac7-8640-ecf0b41f4d06" "answer-78fc0312-8634-566a-bd85-e6da768f8465"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32606e7b-b40b-50e3-a1d1-5d1910806be4" :answer/id #uuid "32606e7b-b40b-50e3-a1d1-5d1910806be4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "370" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be134ae9-dcf1-5a55-b9fd-d851e56132fb" :answer/id #uuid "be134ae9-dcf1-5a55-b9fd-d851e56132fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "390" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-011df462-573f-5d1e-a6c9-530e8e4108d9" :answer/id #uuid "011df462-573f-5d1e-a6c9-530e8e4108d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "400" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90057f88-a3e0-5ac7-8640-ecf0b41f4d06" :answer/id #uuid "90057f88-a3e0-5ac7-8640-ecf0b41f4d06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "430" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78fc0312-8634-566a-bd85-e6da768f8465" :answer/id #uuid "78fc0312-8634-566a-bd85-e6da768f8465" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "440" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7afac99d-b74c-555b-b269-d08cbbf29447" :question/id #uuid "7afac99d-b74c-555b-b269-d08cbbf29447" :question/math-academy-id "q-274686" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)+2=0,$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,{x}_{3}=$ \n{{field-3}}$,\\,{x}_{4}=$ \n{{field-4}}$,\\,{x}_{5}=$ \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)+2 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =\\pi +2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{\\pi +2n\\pi }{5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{5}.$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{5}.$\n\n- Setting $n=4$ gives $x=\\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ \n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{5},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{5},\\,{x}_{3}=\\pi ,\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{7\\pi }{5},\\,{x}_{5}=\\frac{9\\pi }{5}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5825b711-dd97-5566-92cd-e47071c8a90b" "answer-field-b908beae-827a-5298-91c7-8d25daaa56eb" "answer-field-e4ca8561-971c-5261-a117-e700d3f41a23" "answer-field-68851a30-6b06-525c-87ba-9cd42f2bfcd2" "answer-field-998db359-a317-5416-956a-7cdc00ea3436"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5825b711-dd97-5566-92cd-e47071c8a90b" :answer-field/id #uuid "5825b711-dd97-5566-92cd-e47071c8a90b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ed8ee43d-321d-5eba-941d-0c34f9da8b72" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ed8ee43d-321d-5eba-941d-0c34f9da8b72"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed8ee43d-321d-5eba-941d-0c34f9da8b72" :answer/id #uuid "ed8ee43d-321d-5eba-941d-0c34f9da8b72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b908beae-827a-5298-91c7-8d25daaa56eb" :answer-field/id #uuid "b908beae-827a-5298-91c7-8d25daaa56eb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-fe5b3ed0-b28b-5660-bf5d-280988f431a3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fe5b3ed0-b28b-5660-bf5d-280988f431a3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe5b3ed0-b28b-5660-bf5d-280988f431a3" :answer/id #uuid "fe5b3ed0-b28b-5660-bf5d-280988f431a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e4ca8561-971c-5261-a117-e700d3f41a23" :answer-field/id #uuid "e4ca8561-971c-5261-a117-e700d3f41a23" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7df8d642-eb2b-5117-a7ea-b486589f0caf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7df8d642-eb2b-5117-a7ea-b486589f0caf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7df8d642-eb2b-5117-a7ea-b486589f0caf" :answer/id #uuid "7df8d642-eb2b-5117-a7ea-b486589f0caf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-68851a30-6b06-525c-87ba-9cd42f2bfcd2" :answer-field/id #uuid "68851a30-6b06-525c-87ba-9cd42f2bfcd2" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6f7e33ec-3f20-578c-a4d4-eca6b37185da" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6f7e33ec-3f20-578c-a4d4-eca6b37185da"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f7e33ec-3f20-578c-a4d4-eca6b37185da" :answer/id #uuid "6f7e33ec-3f20-578c-a4d4-eca6b37185da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-998db359-a317-5416-956a-7cdc00ea3436" :answer-field/id #uuid "998db359-a317-5416-956a-7cdc00ea3436" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d48a7458-5bf4-56a0-8ae3-12882ba2219d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d48a7458-5bf4-56a0-8ae3-12882ba2219d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d48a7458-5bf4-56a0-8ae3-12882ba2219d" :answer/id #uuid "d48a7458-5bf4-56a0-8ae3-12882ba2219d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c08c7d82-b9b5-59ff-a4f6-2f1eac5d7e57" :question/id #uuid "c08c7d82-b9b5-59ff-a4f6-2f1eac5d7e57" :question/math-academy-id "q-121537" :question/problem "Consider the equation $2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2})+3=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{5}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2})+3 & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}) & =-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}) & =-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x}{2} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & \\frac{5x}{2} & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 5x & =\\frac{7\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{4n\\pi }{5} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{7\\pi }{15}+\\frac{4n\\pi }{5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(12n+5)\\pi }{15} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(12n+7)\\pi }{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{15}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{17\\pi }{15}$  and $x=\\frac{19\\pi }{15}.$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{29\\pi }{15}$  and $x=\\frac{31\\pi }{15}.$ We reject the solution $\\frac{31\\pi }{15}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},{x}_{5}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{15},\\,\\frac{17\\pi }{15},\\,\\frac{19\\pi }{15},\\,\\frac{29\\pi }{15}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{17\\pi }{15}=\\frac{17{\\pi }^{2}}{45}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-babdd4ab-5b3d-5b5f-8892-4e5632d1283f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-babdd4ab-5b3d-5b5f-8892-4e5632d1283f" :answer-field/id #uuid "babdd4ab-5b3d-5b5f-8892-4e5632d1283f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9cd4b999-29de-5078-b62a-6736401df8ee" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3adfe199-8aa8-54b5-af09-00ff18169df0" "answer-b72530e6-e6de-50bd-a6e2-1633d11cef2b" "answer-9cd4b999-29de-5078-b62a-6736401df8ee" "answer-24d2769f-5f35-5619-a06a-6da4d0cd9d06" "answer-66f3e329-d6ad-5cda-b6ea-849f556393e4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3adfe199-8aa8-54b5-af09-00ff18169df0" :answer/id #uuid "3adfe199-8aa8-54b5-af09-00ff18169df0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11{\\pi }^{2}}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b72530e6-e6de-50bd-a6e2-1633d11cef2b" :answer/id #uuid "b72530e6-e6de-50bd-a6e2-1633d11cef2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11{\\pi }^{2}}{45}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9cd4b999-29de-5078-b62a-6736401df8ee" :answer/id #uuid "9cd4b999-29de-5078-b62a-6736401df8ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{17{\\pi }^{2}}{45}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24d2769f-5f35-5619-a06a-6da4d0cd9d06" :answer/id #uuid "24d2769f-5f35-5619-a06a-6da4d0cd9d06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{24{\\pi }^{2}}{45}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-66f3e329-d6ad-5cda-b6ea-849f556393e4" :answer/id #uuid "66f3e329-d6ad-5cda-b6ea-849f556393e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8{\\pi }^{2}}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bf7fd672-fd24-5897-b0f7-1572fe68c96d" :question/id #uuid "bf7fd672-fd24-5897-b0f7-1572fe68c96d" :question/math-academy-id "q-339149" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $cos\\,(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2},$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}, $\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\cdot 2\\pi , & \\, & \\, & x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+n\\cdot 2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\cdot 2\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\cdot 2\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+n\\cdot 2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\nSo, the solutions to the equation, in ascending order, are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e477022f-fdfc-5eff-a7bd-6ef731fb7877" "answer-field-a7620e10-48ae-5504-a96e-87ea7b96af22"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e477022f-fdfc-5eff-a7bd-6ef731fb7877" :answer-field/id #uuid "e477022f-fdfc-5eff-a7bd-6ef731fb7877" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-580a354e-1779-5718-b38a-6a25da50d38b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-580a354e-1779-5718-b38a-6a25da50d38b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-580a354e-1779-5718-b38a-6a25da50d38b" :answer/id #uuid "580a354e-1779-5718-b38a-6a25da50d38b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a7620e10-48ae-5504-a96e-87ea7b96af22" :answer-field/id #uuid "a7620e10-48ae-5504-a96e-87ea7b96af22" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7574b035-fb76-58c1-aaf3-b6389076e7bb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7574b035-fb76-58c1-aaf3-b6389076e7bb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7574b035-fb76-58c1-aaf3-b6389076e7bb" :answer/id #uuid "7574b035-fb76-58c1-aaf3-b6389076e7bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-44457776-708f-510a-afa3-90718ee8b770" :question/id #uuid "44457776-708f-510a-afa3-90718ee8b770" :question/math-academy-id "q-65968" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order,  what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & 2x & ={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{120}^{∘}}{2}+\\frac{n\\cdot {360}^{∘}}{2} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{{240}^{∘}}{2}+\\frac{n\\cdot {360}^{∘}}{2} \\\\ x & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={60}^{∘}$ and $x={120}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={240}^{∘}$ and $x={300}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx={60}^{∘},\\,{120}^{∘},\\,{240}^{∘},\\,{300}^{∘}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}={60}^{∘}$ and ${x}_{3}={240}^{∘},$ the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$ is $60\\cdot 240=14\\,400.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a30da6f7-4089-5e7e-8e3b-203eef88447f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a30da6f7-4089-5e7e-8e3b-203eef88447f" :answer-field/id #uuid "a30da6f7-4089-5e7e-8e3b-203eef88447f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-97a93b64-15dd-5069-a484-3b6fbc1f93e9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-97a93b64-15dd-5069-a484-3b6fbc1f93e9" "answer-504bef0b-52d3-50be-91b2-f61bc23375e2" "answer-73e4e320-b54b-57f9-8435-ec96b175b323" "answer-167b02e4-ac13-5d64-88eb-49c8c9c919f3" "answer-b99395a0-9e8a-5e48-8de9-94bf037a2e4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97a93b64-15dd-5069-a484-3b6fbc1f93e9" :answer/id #uuid "97a93b64-15dd-5069-a484-3b6fbc1f93e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14\\,400" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-504bef0b-52d3-50be-91b2-f61bc23375e2" :answer/id #uuid "504bef0b-52d3-50be-91b2-f61bc23375e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73e4e320-b54b-57f9-8435-ec96b175b323" :answer/id #uuid "73e4e320-b54b-57f9-8435-ec96b175b323" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "28\\,800" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-167b02e4-ac13-5d64-88eb-49c8c9c919f3" :answer/id #uuid "167b02e4-ac13-5d64-88eb-49c8c9c919f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "72\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b99395a0-9e8a-5e48-8de9-94bf037a2e4c" :answer/id #uuid "b99395a0-9e8a-5e48-8de9-94bf037a2e4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\,200" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0663d30d-9045-55b3-9c5e-7d0dcf4eda51" :question/id #uuid "0663d30d-9045-55b3-9c5e-7d0dcf4eda51" :question/math-academy-id "q-335497" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2}-{60}^{∘})+\\frac{3}{2}=0,$ for $0\\le x<{360}^{∘},$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n {{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2}-{60}^{∘})+\\frac{3}{2} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2}-{60}^{∘}) & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2}-{60}^{∘}) & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{2}-{60}^{∘} & ={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & \\frac{3x}{2}-{60}^{∘} & ={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{2} & ={120}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & \\frac{3x}{2} & ={240}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{3x}{2} & ={180}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & \\frac{3x}{2} & ={300}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & ={360}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 3x & ={600}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={200}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain\n$0\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={120}^{∘},$ and $x={200}^{∘}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={120}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={200}^{∘}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-878e4009-95f9-5dc4-a0a9-39ce168a65ce" "answer-field-7779d086-41e5-5d94-b000-b1baac985d4d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-878e4009-95f9-5dc4-a0a9-39ce168a65ce" :answer-field/id #uuid "878e4009-95f9-5dc4-a0a9-39ce168a65ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f6c5a7c9-066c-5198-a3ef-fbb1ede3de63" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f6c5a7c9-066c-5198-a3ef-fbb1ede3de63"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6c5a7c9-066c-5198-a3ef-fbb1ede3de63" :answer/id #uuid "f6c5a7c9-066c-5198-a3ef-fbb1ede3de63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "120" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7779d086-41e5-5d94-b000-b1baac985d4d" :answer-field/id #uuid "7779d086-41e5-5d94-b000-b1baac985d4d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c2590cfe-0743-5c78-9389-41aa7df8f373" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c2590cfe-0743-5c78-9389-41aa7df8f373"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2590cfe-0743-5c78-9389-41aa7df8f373" :answer/id #uuid "c2590cfe-0743-5c78-9389-41aa7df8f373" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "200" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-689ab27d-73f0-5878-911e-f4bfe8b4c90e" :question/id #uuid "689ab27d-73f0-5878-911e-f4bfe8b4c90e" :question/math-academy-id "q-335752" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x+{20}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{2},$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ are ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},{x}_{5},{x}_{6},{x}_{7},$ and ${x}_{8}.$ Determine ${x}_{3},$ ${x}_{6},$ and ${x}_{8}.$\n\n$\\,{x}_{3}=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{6}=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{8}=$ \n{{field-3}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+{20}^{∘} & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & 4x+{20}^{∘} & ={300}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ={60}^{∘}-{20}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 4x & ={300}^{∘}-{20}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ 4x & ={40}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 4x & ={280}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={10}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={70}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={10}^{∘}$ and $x={70}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={100}^{∘}$ and $x={160}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x={190}^{∘}$ and $x={250}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x={280}^{∘}$ and $x={340}^{∘}.$\n\nAll other integer values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ \n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={10}^{∘},\\, & {x}_{2} & ={70}^{∘},\\, & {x}_{3} & ={100}^{∘}, & {x}_{4} & ={160}^{∘},\\, \\\\ {x}_{5} & ={190}^{∘},\\, & {x}_{6} & ={250}^{∘},\\, & {x}_{7} & ={280}^{∘}, & {x}_{8} & ={340}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ec0e8f49-f5e9-53fe-ab58-580c2c1ca697" "answer-field-1e2bf51a-4f30-5939-a652-759a030f310a" "answer-field-f8f063af-1dfe-5ae0-9262-3faf246fb3e4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ec0e8f49-f5e9-53fe-ab58-580c2c1ca697" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec0e8f49-f5e9-53fe-ab58-580c2c1ca697" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1dfb5ef9-f5ff-55e0-8809-ca5e77424e40" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1dfb5ef9-f5ff-55e0-8809-ca5e77424e40"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1dfb5ef9-f5ff-55e0-8809-ca5e77424e40" :answer/id #uuid "1dfb5ef9-f5ff-55e0-8809-ca5e77424e40" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "100" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1e2bf51a-4f30-5939-a652-759a030f310a" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e2bf51a-4f30-5939-a652-759a030f310a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e7049029-dac1-5b25-b0a9-4b9ed11b7bb8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e7049029-dac1-5b25-b0a9-4b9ed11b7bb8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7049029-dac1-5b25-b0a9-4b9ed11b7bb8" :answer/id #uuid "e7049029-dac1-5b25-b0a9-4b9ed11b7bb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "250" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f8f063af-1dfe-5ae0-9262-3faf246fb3e4" :answer-field/id #uuid "f8f063af-1dfe-5ae0-9262-3faf246fb3e4" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6b7f9c83-02c1-5e70-9da1-af9c14a34e35" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6b7f9c83-02c1-5e70-9da1-af9c14a34e35"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b7f9c83-02c1-5e70-9da1-af9c14a34e35" :answer/id #uuid "6b7f9c83-02c1-5e70-9da1-af9c14a34e35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "340" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cd740014-1051-58c1-bf02-d54c44856f2e" :question/id #uuid "cd740014-1051-58c1-bf02-d54c44856f2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-8416" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{(1+12n)\\pi }{12} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(3+4n)\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{13\\pi }{12}$  and $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{13\\pi }{12},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{13\\pi }{12}=\\frac{13{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-07364bf1-9117-523c-8ac5-3757ab4f12da"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-07364bf1-9117-523c-8ac5-3757ab4f12da" :answer-field/id #uuid "07364bf1-9117-523c-8ac5-3757ab4f12da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-243b413f-1ed5-5fd9-83ef-c67f2fb483ac" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bc1c9710-16d0-52b3-9cf5-cd2bf56b2518" "answer-243b413f-1ed5-5fd9-83ef-c67f2fb483ac" "answer-ab0d6bfa-f34f-56b8-b82f-bb5140952e0d" "answer-bf8999d6-066a-50d5-a8fc-0ad42db04a2f" "answer-022e0018-2f42-5bc0-839a-94b3b6a7345a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc1c9710-16d0-52b3-9cf5-cd2bf56b2518" :answer/id #uuid "bc1c9710-16d0-52b3-9cf5-cd2bf56b2518" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{13{\\pi }^{2}}{144}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-243b413f-1ed5-5fd9-83ef-c67f2fb483ac" :answer/id #uuid "243b413f-1ed5-5fd9-83ef-c67f2fb483ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{13{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab0d6bfa-f34f-56b8-b82f-bb5140952e0d" :answer/id #uuid "ab0d6bfa-f34f-56b8-b82f-bb5140952e0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{13{\\pi }^{2}}{48}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf8999d6-066a-50d5-a8fc-0ad42db04a2f" :answer/id #uuid "bf8999d6-066a-50d5-a8fc-0ad42db04a2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{144}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-022e0018-2f42-5bc0-839a-94b3b6a7345a" :answer/id #uuid "022e0018-2f42-5bc0-839a-94b3b6a7345a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{48}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-468c5cf8-2c4e-5dd6-96eb-d94e2d3f4648" :question/id #uuid "468c5cf8-2c4e-5dd6-96eb-d94e2d3f4648" :question/math-academy-id "q-335765" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+{30}^{∘})-2=0,$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ are ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},{x}_{5},$ and ${x}_{6}.$ Determine ${x}_{2},$ ${x}_{4},$ and ${x}_{6}.$\n\n$\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{6}=$ \n{{field-3}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+{30}^{∘})-2 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+{30}^{∘}) & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+{30}^{∘}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+{30}^{∘} & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & 3x+{30}^{∘} & ={300}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & ={60}^{∘}-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 3x & ={300}^{∘}-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 3x & ={270}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={10}^{∘}+n\\cdot {120}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={10}^{∘}$ and $x={90}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={130}^{∘}$ and $x={210}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x={250}^{∘}$ and $x={330}^{∘}.$\n\nAll other integer values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$  \n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={10}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={90}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}={130}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}={210}^{∘},\\,{x}_{5}={250}^{∘},\\,{x}_{6}={330}^{∘}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3541aadb-cc54-5704-a42d-4d1a7872ed3b" "answer-field-a66d7b3d-8473-5ef9-a750-65b48209e520" "answer-field-bd5fb88d-f9cd-588b-9d6c-0e9acff9fa9f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3541aadb-cc54-5704-a42d-4d1a7872ed3b" :answer-field/id #uuid "3541aadb-cc54-5704-a42d-4d1a7872ed3b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-845d5508-0277-5506-af04-f8743869a08e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-845d5508-0277-5506-af04-f8743869a08e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-845d5508-0277-5506-af04-f8743869a08e" :answer/id #uuid "845d5508-0277-5506-af04-f8743869a08e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "90" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a66d7b3d-8473-5ef9-a750-65b48209e520" :answer-field/id #uuid "a66d7b3d-8473-5ef9-a750-65b48209e520" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b482e662-fd2a-50e9-89dd-a5931b7d5547" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b482e662-fd2a-50e9-89dd-a5931b7d5547"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b482e662-fd2a-50e9-89dd-a5931b7d5547" :answer/id #uuid "b482e662-fd2a-50e9-89dd-a5931b7d5547" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "210" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bd5fb88d-f9cd-588b-9d6c-0e9acff9fa9f" :answer-field/id #uuid "bd5fb88d-f9cd-588b-9d6c-0e9acff9fa9f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-df447177-ab9b-5f56-8c5d-57b790680d2e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-df447177-ab9b-5f56-8c5d-57b790680d2e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df447177-ab9b-5f56-8c5d-57b790680d2e" :answer/id #uuid "df447177-ab9b-5f56-8c5d-57b790680d2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "330" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9acc1f61-f727-5595-afa3-5b687cb64df5" :question/id #uuid "9acc1f61-f727-5595-afa3-5b687cb64df5" :question/math-academy-id "q-8471" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{7\\pi }{6} & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 2x-\\frac{7\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{11\\pi }{12}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{(4n+1)\\pi }{4} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(12n+11)\\pi }{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{11\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{23\\pi }{12}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$  So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{12},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{23\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{23\\pi }{12}=\\frac{23{\\pi }^{2}}{48}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-82b1eb6b-29b6-5c06-8aaf-865126a7825b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-82b1eb6b-29b6-5c06-8aaf-865126a7825b" :answer-field/id #uuid "82b1eb6b-29b6-5c06-8aaf-865126a7825b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e0a0c041-ac86-5d7d-b144-83b28e3c689b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-43b5a5a3-e3bd-5ca5-abf0-99897ef0d6ed" "answer-482140ef-4bcd-5c73-b1a5-1371712b352b" "answer-e0a0c041-ac86-5d7d-b144-83b28e3c689b" "answer-9a3f101b-39b0-5f18-bb20-2ed53c584e10" "answer-d8f3b022-ee82-5c9f-a98e-79cfe7d39c0b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43b5a5a3-e3bd-5ca5-abf0-99897ef0d6ed" :answer/id #uuid "43b5a5a3-e3bd-5ca5-abf0-99897ef0d6ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-482140ef-4bcd-5c73-b1a5-1371712b352b" :answer/id #uuid "482140ef-4bcd-5c73-b1a5-1371712b352b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0a0c041-ac86-5d7d-b144-83b28e3c689b" :answer/id #uuid "e0a0c041-ac86-5d7d-b144-83b28e3c689b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{23{\\pi }^{2}}{48}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a3f101b-39b0-5f18-bb20-2ed53c584e10" :answer/id #uuid "9a3f101b-39b0-5f18-bb20-2ed53c584e10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{48}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8f3b022-ee82-5c9f-a98e-79cfe7d39c0b" :answer/id #uuid "d8f3b022-ee82-5c9f-a98e-79cfe7d39c0b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8d85e958-8094-54f0-80e0-53c48caaf572" :question/id #uuid "8d85e958-8094-54f0-80e0-53c48caaf572" :question/math-academy-id "q-338812" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+2=0,$ for $0<x<\\pi ,$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,{x}_{3}=$ \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+2 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+n\\cdot 2\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 3x-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+n\\cdot 2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\cdot 2\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 3x & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+n\\cdot 2\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 3x & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+n\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{18}+n\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0<x<\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{18}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{17\\pi }{18}$ and $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$  We reject the solution $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0<x<\\pi .$ \n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{18},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,{x}_{3}=\\frac{17\\pi }{18}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4850e080-9da6-5a10-9e66-b565a38821ca" "answer-field-1480127b-3372-528c-becd-c6fd13cbb58d" "answer-field-431ae314-3de9-59e8-bfd8-a5f31a6afc22"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4850e080-9da6-5a10-9e66-b565a38821ca" :answer-field/id #uuid "4850e080-9da6-5a10-9e66-b565a38821ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a061380a-b6d7-5415-aa51-6d76659ce87d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a061380a-b6d7-5415-aa51-6d76659ce87d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a061380a-b6d7-5415-aa51-6d76659ce87d" :answer/id #uuid "a061380a-b6d7-5415-aa51-6d76659ce87d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{18}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1480127b-3372-528c-becd-c6fd13cbb58d" :answer-field/id #uuid "1480127b-3372-528c-becd-c6fd13cbb58d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6d90f992-2297-5d05-ac55-9ba4508a814d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6d90f992-2297-5d05-ac55-9ba4508a814d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d90f992-2297-5d05-ac55-9ba4508a814d" :answer/id #uuid "6d90f992-2297-5d05-ac55-9ba4508a814d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-431ae314-3de9-59e8-bfd8-a5f31a6afc22" :answer-field/id #uuid "431ae314-3de9-59e8-bfd8-a5f31a6afc22" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9c63190b-23bc-5290-8d3f-231a362b63e6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9c63190b-23bc-5290-8d3f-231a362b63e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c63190b-23bc-5290-8d3f-231a362b63e6" :answer/id #uuid "9c63190b-23bc-5290-8d3f-231a362b63e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{17\\pi}{18}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f8328338-c374-57d6-bab7-26f0397ea50b" :question/id #uuid "f8328338-c374-57d6-bab7-26f0397ea50b" :question/math-academy-id "q-65971" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=\\frac{1}{2}$ for $0\\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{(6n-1)\\pi }{6} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(6n+1)\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ We reject the solution $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$ We reject the solution $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{6}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7432eaab-38b7-5b84-9d2a-70e7ae9a23c4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7432eaab-38b7-5b84-9d2a-70e7ae9a23c4" :answer-field/id #uuid "7432eaab-38b7-5b84-9d2a-70e7ae9a23c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d30ec1f8-9a77-5358-8c53-8e66abf6be5d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1370b931-f307-514b-acc4-b0c230734c28" "answer-52f06248-697b-5956-9ee8-1508ba5f0b5e" "answer-45a03d7c-c8d6-5f14-af62-73e1fcf2af10" "answer-1b1a8038-c551-5e26-9b1f-f7016b6e0545" "answer-d30ec1f8-9a77-5358-8c53-8e66abf6be5d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1370b931-f307-514b-acc4-b0c230734c28" :answer/id #uuid "1370b931-f307-514b-acc4-b0c230734c28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52f06248-697b-5956-9ee8-1508ba5f0b5e" :answer/id #uuid "52f06248-697b-5956-9ee8-1508ba5f0b5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{25}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45a03d7c-c8d6-5f14-af62-73e1fcf2af10" :answer/id #uuid "45a03d7c-c8d6-5f14-af62-73e1fcf2af10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b1a8038-c551-5e26-9b1f-f7016b6e0545" :answer/id #uuid "1b1a8038-c551-5e26-9b1f-f7016b6e0545" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d30ec1f8-9a77-5358-8c53-8e66abf6be5d" :answer/id #uuid "d30ec1f8-9a77-5358-8c53-8e66abf6be5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ef6f56bf-b6a0-53ab-9821-d8f13185e30a" :question/id #uuid "ef6f56bf-b6a0-53ab-9821-d8f13185e30a" :question/math-academy-id "q-65970" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ for $0\\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{12}+n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{12}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{(12n-5)\\pi }{12} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(12n+5)\\pi }{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.$ We reject the solution $-\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}$ and $x=\\frac{17\\pi }{12}.$ We reject the solution $\\frac{17\\pi }{12}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{12}=\\frac{35{\\pi }^{2}}{144}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-991d8fa8-582c-5364-b708-a7dbf249588d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-991d8fa8-582c-5364-b708-a7dbf249588d" :answer-field/id #uuid "991d8fa8-582c-5364-b708-a7dbf249588d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-15bd6583-d234-5634-9629-19b45df33048" :answer-field/choices ["answer-15bd6583-d234-5634-9629-19b45df33048" "answer-b76b1697-f9b4-5319-9e00-35a7fd2da032" "answer-9145a808-7c66-5308-8a18-4bcd731ea879" "answer-aa868f03-9cf5-5643-ae39-66d4807c5346" "answer-5bf9f519-7e9a-582e-ba24-2db80b66b0c8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15bd6583-d234-5634-9629-19b45df33048" :answer/id #uuid "15bd6583-d234-5634-9629-19b45df33048" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{35{\\pi }^{2}}{144}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b76b1697-f9b4-5319-9e00-35a7fd2da032" :answer/id #uuid "b76b1697-f9b4-5319-9e00-35a7fd2da032" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{25}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9145a808-7c66-5308-8a18-4bcd731ea879" :answer/id #uuid "9145a808-7c66-5308-8a18-4bcd731ea879" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa868f03-9cf5-5643-ae39-66d4807c5346" :answer/id #uuid "aa868f03-9cf5-5643-ae39-66d4807c5346" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5bf9f519-7e9a-582e-ba24-2db80b66b0c8" :answer/id #uuid "5bf9f519-7e9a-582e-ba24-2db80b66b0c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-637ba677-860c-50d3-9633-f9db8d1adb0f" :question/id #uuid "637ba677-860c-50d3-9633-f9db8d1adb0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-335807" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+{30}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{2},$ for $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘},$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}+{30}^{∘} & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{2}+{30}^{∘} & ={300}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & ={60}^{∘}-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{2} & ={300}^{∘}-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{2} & ={270}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={540}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-{660}^{∘}$ and $x=-{180}^{∘}.$  We reject the solution $-{660}^{∘}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={60}^{∘}$ and $x={540}^{∘}.$  We reject the solution ${540}^{∘}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘}.$ \n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={-180}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={60}^{∘}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-deb66c26-5f33-5200-8bd7-282e210c4347" "answer-field-ada90f2e-5064-56a4-aabd-120c6b51216d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-deb66c26-5f33-5200-8bd7-282e210c4347" :answer-field/id #uuid "deb66c26-5f33-5200-8bd7-282e210c4347" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d9a00334-4f57-5b02-bfff-2b787d85af60" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d9a00334-4f57-5b02-bfff-2b787d85af60"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9a00334-4f57-5b02-bfff-2b787d85af60" :answer/id #uuid "d9a00334-4f57-5b02-bfff-2b787d85af60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-180" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ada90f2e-5064-56a4-aabd-120c6b51216d" :answer-field/id #uuid "ada90f2e-5064-56a4-aabd-120c6b51216d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1aa4f820-013c-52fa-b244-38fafa8db366" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1aa4f820-013c-52fa-b244-38fafa8db366"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1aa4f820-013c-52fa-b244-38fafa8db366" :answer/id #uuid "1aa4f820-013c-52fa-b244-38fafa8db366" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "60" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-af242b6e-a0fa-5102-becb-649e55619922" :question/id #uuid "af242b6e-a0fa-5102-becb-649e55619922" :question/math-academy-id "q-8422" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for $0\\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{12}+n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{(12n-5)\\pi }{12} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(4n+1)\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ We reject $-\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$ We reject $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0<x\\le \\pi .$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{12}=\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{48}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c6635c31-8a89-581f-88ba-c9350baa70ca"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c6635c31-8a89-581f-88ba-c9350baa70ca" :answer-field/id #uuid "c6635c31-8a89-581f-88ba-c9350baa70ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-edf9637c-aede-513e-8154-e840c0b4d689" :answer-field/choices ["answer-da5f6ffe-39d2-5021-9518-42610f548da9" "answer-33d9c60f-2f9d-53f4-980f-9aa97023962f" "answer-edf9637c-aede-513e-8154-e840c0b4d689" "answer-d7168513-56cb-58e5-b423-239ea3770fba" "answer-18eb997a-9844-5aac-b91f-00f5f300d39b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da5f6ffe-39d2-5021-9518-42610f548da9" :answer/id #uuid "da5f6ffe-39d2-5021-9518-42610f548da9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33d9c60f-2f9d-53f4-980f-9aa97023962f" :answer/id #uuid "33d9c60f-2f9d-53f4-980f-9aa97023962f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{24}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-edf9637c-aede-513e-8154-e840c0b4d689" :answer/id #uuid "edf9637c-aede-513e-8154-e840c0b4d689" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{48}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7168513-56cb-58e5-b423-239ea3770fba" :answer/id #uuid "d7168513-56cb-58e5-b423-239ea3770fba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{48}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18eb997a-9844-5aac-b91f-00f5f300d39b" :answer/id #uuid "18eb997a-9844-5aac-b91f-00f5f300d39b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{24}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093391 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{2}, \n\t\\qquad -\\pi \\leq x < \\pi.\n$$\n\nNotice that the equation contains a transformed cosine function. \n\nWe know that the general solution to the elementary equation $\\cos{X} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & X & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is assumed to be any integer.\n\nTo find the general solution of the transformed cosine equation, we replace $X$ with the input of the cosine function, which is $x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ in this case. This gives the general solution \n\n$$\nx+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + 2n\\pi, \\qquad\\qquad x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}+ 2n\\pi.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above two equations for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi  \\\\  &  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+\\frac{6n\\pi }{3} \\\\  &  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{2\\pi (2+3n)}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $n = 0, \\pm1, \\pm2, \\ldots$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi \\leq x < \\pi.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=0$ and $x=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}.$ We reject the solution $\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-2\\pi$ and $x=-\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$ We reject the solution $-2\\pi$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi \\leq x < \\pi.$ So, the solutions $x_1$ and $x_2$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 = -\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},\\qquad x_2 = 0.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092424 :activity/expected-seconds 1320.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176033 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d6509173-6968-54cb-999d-d5fe096ebbff" "question-f1b17b2b-5f71-5d88-80f9-025ec7f6d205" "question-b2848e9c-e485-5752-8daf-a5d57b496ccb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d6509173-6968-54cb-999d-d5fe096ebbff" :question/id #uuid "d6509173-6968-54cb-999d-d5fe096ebbff" :question/math-academy-id "q-134798" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b375c1e162a9f1c2892903c20606921673498579f9b41f633447832fd3ea2192.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for the data sets $x$ and $y$ of paired observations and the corresponding linear regression line are shown above.\n\nAccording to the regression model, what is the residual for the highlighted datapoint?" :question/worked-solution "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe highlighted datapoint has coordinates $(4,70).$ So, our actual value is $y=70.$\n\nOn the other hand, the point on the regression line that corresponds to $x=4$ has the $y$-coordinate $50.$ So, our estimated value of is $\\overset{^}{y}=50.$\n\n![](images/04/041f64255345edf7ac37bd61e0ab97a93cc2696f028a53ba0896e0659966c2d8.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the residual is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =70-50 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b8c336d7-1f88-5669-a3cc-3ac8dd456feb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b8c336d7-1f88-5669-a3cc-3ac8dd456feb" :answer-field/id #uuid "b8c336d7-1f88-5669-a3cc-3ac8dd456feb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ba0a7473-8f7d-5f9a-97c9-726d94e6f4b6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6dfd5478-a857-5295-91e8-3b663a1bc7a7" "answer-ba0a7473-8f7d-5f9a-97c9-726d94e6f4b6" "answer-00765988-b24e-516e-81a5-803465b2cbab" "answer-2fb61cf0-8148-5e19-85df-5a17cfbc10f6" "answer-f0b10261-e8cd-557e-a60d-49043c55ee7c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6dfd5478-a857-5295-91e8-3b663a1bc7a7" :answer/id #uuid "6dfd5478-a857-5295-91e8-3b663a1bc7a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba0a7473-8f7d-5f9a-97c9-726d94e6f4b6" :answer/id #uuid "ba0a7473-8f7d-5f9a-97c9-726d94e6f4b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00765988-b24e-516e-81a5-803465b2cbab" :answer/id #uuid "00765988-b24e-516e-81a5-803465b2cbab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fb61cf0-8148-5e19-85df-5a17cfbc10f6" :answer/id #uuid "2fb61cf0-8148-5e19-85df-5a17cfbc10f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0b10261-e8cd-557e-a60d-49043c55ee7c" :answer/id #uuid "f0b10261-e8cd-557e-a60d-49043c55ee7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "35" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f1b17b2b-5f71-5d88-80f9-025ec7f6d205" :question/id #uuid "f1b17b2b-5f71-5d88-80f9-025ec7f6d205" :question/math-academy-id "q-134808" :question/problem "![](images/08/08ce939458d5c298805692df7d10bed2abdcf81ec874da3f61dbe15f59fc141a.png)\n\nA biologist records the heights $y$ (in meters) of several oak trees at certain times $x$ (in years) after they were planted. The scatter plot for the data and the corresponding linear regression line are shown above.  \n\nAccording to the regression model, what is the residual for the highlighted datapoint?" :question/worked-solution "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe highlighted datapoint has coordinates $(6,6.5).$ So, our actual value is $y=6.5.$\n\nOn the other hand, the point on the regression line that corresponds to $x=6$ has the $y$-coordinate $5.5.$ So, our estimated value is $\\overset{^}{y}=5.5.$\n\n![](images/ee/eedd1cadb2ba49fb95f9e18f9bef365942e53521e6894a270c9a1e1474f3ed71.png)\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =6.5-5.5 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residual is $1\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6f4f4e5c-e51e-5cea-9a0b-324f1c69bce1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6f4f4e5c-e51e-5cea-9a0b-324f1c69bce1" :answer-field/id #uuid "6f4f4e5c-e51e-5cea-9a0b-324f1c69bce1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5619b467-5b18-5824-9a47-f5bbd8a4a4a2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2b317e2f-e6dd-53e0-9e49-ee9c188855d2" "answer-b26c54e1-c250-50fe-b5f2-37358d367201" "answer-612936c1-41a2-55be-bcc4-3b8cf0813d4b" "answer-5619b467-5b18-5824-9a47-f5bbd8a4a4a2" "answer-49bc7362-977c-5232-be6c-b5cfdd1ef847"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b317e2f-e6dd-53e0-9e49-ee9c188855d2" :answer/id #uuid "2b317e2f-e6dd-53e0-9e49-ee9c188855d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.5\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b26c54e1-c250-50fe-b5f2-37358d367201" :answer/id #uuid "b26c54e1-c250-50fe-b5f2-37358d367201" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-612936c1-41a2-55be-bcc4-3b8cf0813d4b" :answer/id #uuid "612936c1-41a2-55be-bcc4-3b8cf0813d4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5619b467-5b18-5824-9a47-f5bbd8a4a4a2" :answer/id #uuid "5619b467-5b18-5824-9a47-f5bbd8a4a4a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-49bc7362-977c-5232-be6c-b5cfdd1ef847" :answer/id #uuid "49bc7362-977c-5232-be6c-b5cfdd1ef847" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b2848e9c-e485-5752-8daf-a5d57b496ccb" :question/id #uuid "b2848e9c-e485-5752-8daf-a5d57b496ccb" :question/math-academy-id "q-134812" :question/problem "![](images/24/243a4212d1128a79203493bf672ad1c956fe0f48aad6abbb910de2861d99669c.png)\n\nA dog food company records data on the number of dogs $(x)$ sheltered at some kennels across the country and the corresponding amount of dog food $y$ (in kilograms) consumed in a month. The scatter plot for the data and the corresponding linear regression line are shown above. \n\nAccording to the regression model, what is the residual for the highlighted data point?" :question/worked-solution "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe highlighted datapoint has coordinates $(10,175).$ So, our actual value is $y=175.$\n\nOn the other hand, the point on the regression line that corresponds to $x=10$ has the $y$-coordinate $200.$ So, our estimated value is $\\overset{^}{y}=200.$\n\n![](images/2c/2cd59a9ae9c7ad474a9d5a308d4b8f3c9b96be70acdf1e3613dfc4757ff01766.png)\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =175-200 \\\\  & =-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residual is $-25\\,kg.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9ed40114-2ba1-5996-b505-11a621544fa9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9ed40114-2ba1-5996-b505-11a621544fa9" :answer-field/id #uuid "9ed40114-2ba1-5996-b505-11a621544fa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-32b8dd5f-70e9-5121-9de5-389361fe1956" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6748c281-3aeb-5426-9750-d7329ae49821" "answer-32b8dd5f-70e9-5121-9de5-389361fe1956" "answer-111c354b-41eb-5072-82a1-a27b376ef779" "answer-3eb6331c-9c5e-5112-8172-2d2c6ae715e1" "answer-5ecc3dc4-f5bd-51df-a791-7eef362a46eb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6748c281-3aeb-5426-9750-d7329ae49821" :answer/id #uuid "6748c281-3aeb-5426-9750-d7329ae49821" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-15\\,kg" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32b8dd5f-70e9-5121-9de5-389361fe1956" :answer/id #uuid "32b8dd5f-70e9-5121-9de5-389361fe1956" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-25\\,kg" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-111c354b-41eb-5072-82a1-a27b376ef779" :answer/id #uuid "111c354b-41eb-5072-82a1-a27b376ef779" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15\\,kg" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3eb6331c-9c5e-5112-8172-2d2c6ae715e1" :answer/id #uuid "3eb6331c-9c5e-5112-8172-2d2c6ae715e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20\\,kg" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ecc3dc4-f5bd-51df-a791-7eef362a46eb" :answer/id #uuid "5ecc3dc4-f5bd-51df-a791-7eef362a46eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "50\\,kg" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-ef56f8ca-db40-565e-bd1c-7c7a8fb3e9b0" :activity/id #uuid "ef56f8ca-db40-565e-bd1c-7c7a8fb3e9b0" :activity/title "Residuals and Residual Plots" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050269 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-106ec1c5-5aeb-55e4-9a89-04cf9274a237" "step-328f4cd9-6f09-53f8-b7dd-274af5b0a028" "step-47edabd6-568a-5a2a-87d9-33d6db405e8a"] :activity/first-step "step-106ec1c5-5aeb-55e4-9a89-04cf9274a237" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-106ec1c5-5aeb-55e4-9a89-04cf9274a237" :step/id #uuid "106ec1c5-5aeb-55e4-9a89-04cf9274a237" :step/content "question-d6509173-6968-54cb-999d-d5fe096ebbff" :step/next "step-328f4cd9-6f09-53f8-b7dd-274af5b0a028" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-328f4cd9-6f09-53f8-b7dd-274af5b0a028" :step/id #uuid "328f4cd9-6f09-53f8-b7dd-274af5b0a028" :step/content "question-f1b17b2b-5f71-5d88-80f9-025ec7f6d205" :step/next "step-47edabd6-568a-5a2a-87d9-33d6db405e8a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-47edabd6-568a-5a2a-87d9-33d6db405e8a" :step/id #uuid "47edabd6-568a-5a2a-87d9-33d6db405e8a" :step/content "question-b2848e9c-e485-5752-8daf-a5d57b496ccb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281623 :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.$\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to its distance from the $y$-axis.\nFind the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281670 :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D,$ in polar coordinates, can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=kr,$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}kr\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}k{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\frac{7}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{14\\pi k}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=kr=\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }r.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }r\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{31}{5}\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{93M}{70}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-fbfee663-8b23-548a-9017-c32bfd0341c2" :activity/id #uuid "fbfee663-8b23-548a-9017-c32bfd0341c2" :activity/title "Moments of Inertia of Laminas About the Coordinate Axes" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049331 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-405d19e9-3a88-5093-b8b6-2cd83492a961" "step-d6cee4dd-7238-5329-8232-43c8ab15f703" "step-4861f48e-241a-5189-b7da-de189e45b3fa" "step-492495b0-70ff-526e-826b-5f71c3cc5957" "step-ed4ead03-e386-561a-a146-c0edf88b4c49"] :activity/first-step "step-405d19e9-3a88-5093-b8b6-2cd83492a961" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-405d19e9-3a88-5093-b8b6-2cd83492a961" :step/id #uuid "405d19e9-3a88-5093-b8b6-2cd83492a961" :step/content 17592186129537 :step/next "step-d6cee4dd-7238-5329-8232-43c8ab15f703" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d6cee4dd-7238-5329-8232-43c8ab15f703" :step/id #uuid "d6cee4dd-7238-5329-8232-43c8ab15f703" :step/content 17592186281623 :step/next "step-4861f48e-241a-5189-b7da-de189e45b3fa" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4861f48e-241a-5189-b7da-de189e45b3fa" :step/id #uuid "4861f48e-241a-5189-b7da-de189e45b3fa" :step/content 17592186426166 :step/next "step-492495b0-70ff-526e-826b-5f71c3cc5957" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-492495b0-70ff-526e-826b-5f71c3cc5957" :step/id #uuid "492495b0-70ff-526e-826b-5f71c3cc5957" :step/content 17592186281637 :step/next "step-ed4ead03-e386-561a-a146-c0edf88b4c49" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ed4ead03-e386-561a-a146-c0edf88b4c49" :step/id #uuid "ed4ead03-e386-561a-a146-c0edf88b4c49" :step/content 17592186281670 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184342 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-efcb8d92-e0fd-53fd-9869-69a86351884a" "question-e5a43bfc-062d-59bd-8636-9805b214ca53"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-efcb8d92-e0fd-53fd-9869-69a86351884a" :question/id #uuid "efcb8d92-e0fd-53fd-9869-69a86351884a" :question/math-academy-id "q-3900" :question/problem "Evaluate $f(x)={x}^{2}-6x+9$ at $x=a+2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $f(a+2),$ we write down the function $f$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $(a+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}-6x+9 \\\\ f(a+2) & =(a+2)^{2}-6(a+2)+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a+2) & =(a+2)^{2}-6(a+2)+9 \\\\  & =(a+2)(a+2)-6a-12+9 \\\\  & ={a}^{2}+2a+2a+4-6a-3 \\\\  & ={a}^{2}-2a+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(a+2)={a}^{2}-2a+1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-358c69cd-3ebc-5d97-9a9d-cdbfb1e80a78"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-358c69cd-3ebc-5d97-9a9d-cdbfb1e80a78" :answer-field/id #uuid "358c69cd-3ebc-5d97-9a9d-cdbfb1e80a78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c1970721-3576-5dbe-b614-e6262fbf4c6a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-76a0b15e-8792-5dad-8f77-92d2f58c48fe" "answer-11b1c1bf-2a67-5c91-92bc-0b92c34f4a40" "answer-688799a2-d61c-5052-a6f0-07ce3472dfbb" "answer-4ccd54ce-a52c-5b42-898c-f9760ff781cf" "answer-c1970721-3576-5dbe-b614-e6262fbf4c6a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76a0b15e-8792-5dad-8f77-92d2f58c48fe" :answer/id #uuid "76a0b15e-8792-5dad-8f77-92d2f58c48fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{a}^{2}+2a+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11b1c1bf-2a67-5c91-92bc-0b92c34f4a40" :answer/id #uuid "11b1c1bf-2a67-5c91-92bc-0b92c34f4a40" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{a}^{2}+2a+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-688799a2-d61c-5052-a6f0-07ce3472dfbb" :answer/id #uuid "688799a2-d61c-5052-a6f0-07ce3472dfbb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}^{2}+2a+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ccd54ce-a52c-5b42-898c-f9760ff781cf" :answer/id #uuid "4ccd54ce-a52c-5b42-898c-f9760ff781cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}^{2}+2a-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1970721-3576-5dbe-b614-e6262fbf4c6a" :answer/id #uuid "c1970721-3576-5dbe-b614-e6262fbf4c6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}^{2}-2a+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e5a43bfc-062d-59bd-8636-9805b214ca53" :question/id #uuid "e5a43bfc-062d-59bd-8636-9805b214ca53" :question/math-academy-id "q-66446" :question/problem "If $f(x)={x}^{2}+2x-5$, then $f(2p-1)=$" :question/worked-solution "To find $f(2p-1),$ we write down the function $f$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $(2p-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}+2x-5 \\\\ f(2p-1) & =(2p-1)^{2}+2(2p-1)-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2p-1) & =(2p-1)^{2}+2(2p-1)-5 \\\\  & =(2p-1)(2p-1)+4p-2-5 \\\\  & =4{p}^{2}-2p-2p+1+4p-7 \\\\  & =4{p}^{2}-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(2p-1)=4{p}^{2}-6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-515fa882-da2b-53bc-a0ac-98c42e8cebf3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-515fa882-da2b-53bc-a0ac-98c42e8cebf3" :answer-field/id #uuid "515fa882-da2b-53bc-a0ac-98c42e8cebf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4a7f8e06-5a54-5672-ba21-201d8162c811" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7cd5aebb-db28-53c1-8e40-925f6096e6ee" "answer-32a224a7-1eb4-5777-a6ac-6be22859d307" "answer-dda5c6a8-dd5c-5cd9-87ed-9811dc416ce7" "answer-4a7f8e06-5a54-5672-ba21-201d8162c811" "answer-b514765d-7d04-5bf6-bca0-72cd46f1cbb7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7cd5aebb-db28-53c1-8e40-925f6096e6ee" :answer/id #uuid "7cd5aebb-db28-53c1-8e40-925f6096e6ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{p}^{2}+2p-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32a224a7-1eb4-5777-a6ac-6be22859d307" :answer/id #uuid "32a224a7-1eb4-5777-a6ac-6be22859d307" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{p}^{2}+4p-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dda5c6a8-dd5c-5cd9-87ed-9811dc416ce7" :answer/id #uuid "dda5c6a8-dd5c-5cd9-87ed-9811dc416ce7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{p}^{2}-4p-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a7f8e06-5a54-5672-ba21-201d8162c811" :answer/id #uuid "4a7f8e06-5a54-5672-ba21-201d8162c811" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{p}^{2}-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b514765d-7d04-5bf6-bca0-72cd46f1cbb7" :answer/id #uuid "b514765d-7d04-5bf6-bca0-72cd46f1cbb7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{p}^{2}-8p-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184341 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e71385aa-2fca-5283-a0e8-74b07850b796"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e71385aa-2fca-5283-a0e8-74b07850b796" :question/id #uuid "e71385aa-2fca-5283-a0e8-74b07850b796" :question/math-academy-id "q-222425" :question/problem "If $f(x)=2{x}^{2}-1,$ then $(f∘f)(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $(f∘f)(x)$ is equivalent to $f(f(x)).$\n\nTo find $f(f(x)),$ we write down the function $f$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =2{x}^{2}-1 \\\\ f(f(x)) & =2(f(x))^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $f(x)=2{x}^{2}-1$ into the right-hand side of the above and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(f(x)) & =2(f(x))^{2}-1 \\\\  & =2(2{x}^{2}-1)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =2(4{x}^{4}-4{x}^{2}+1)-1 \\\\  & =8{x}^{4}-8{x}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(f∘f)(x)=8{x}^{4}-8{x}^{2}+1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-205ef3eb-ccfc-5714-a196-5890ad4baa93"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-205ef3eb-ccfc-5714-a196-5890ad4baa93" :answer-field/id #uuid "205ef3eb-ccfc-5714-a196-5890ad4baa93" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-abd7f3ea-e1df-5aa2-b07e-3e4d14c0cfb6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-abd7f3ea-e1df-5aa2-b07e-3e4d14c0cfb6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-abd7f3ea-e1df-5aa2-b07e-3e4d14c0cfb6" :answer/id #uuid "abd7f3ea-e1df-5aa2-b07e-3e4d14c0cfb6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8x^{4}-8x^{2}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-a7028d09-7fe3-5f85-985d-69463f19127c" :activity/id #uuid "a7028d09-7fe3-5f85-985d-69463f19127c" :activity/title "Describing Function Composition" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049317 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-1fa886ed-c413-5138-87d7-64f1b6254029" "step-0f6227d1-426e-593d-88db-a2fa0e5e0738" "step-a94f4e57-f0e0-5cad-b2db-64e85028ec05"] :activity/first-step "step-1fa886ed-c413-5138-87d7-64f1b6254029" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-1fa886ed-c413-5138-87d7-64f1b6254029" :step/id #uuid "1fa886ed-c413-5138-87d7-64f1b6254029" :step/content "question-efcb8d92-e0fd-53fd-9869-69a86351884a" :step/next "step-0f6227d1-426e-593d-88db-a2fa0e5e0738" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0f6227d1-426e-593d-88db-a2fa0e5e0738" :step/id #uuid "0f6227d1-426e-593d-88db-a2fa0e5e0738" :step/content "question-e5a43bfc-062d-59bd-8636-9805b214ca53" :step/next "step-a94f4e57-f0e0-5cad-b2db-64e85028ec05" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a94f4e57-f0e0-5cad-b2db-64e85028ec05" :step/id #uuid "a94f4e57-f0e0-5cad-b2db-64e85028ec05" :step/content "question-e71385aa-2fca-5283-a0e8-74b07850b796" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068746 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-733ab756-eb23-5365-83ae-dfce071139ee" "question-82176286-a684-5858-a421-cc4a4b4187bd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-733ab756-eb23-5365-83ae-dfce071139ee" :question/id #uuid "733ab756-eb23-5365-83ae-dfce071139ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-3600" :question/problem "Factor $\\frac{1}{27}+{z}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "The sum of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}+{b}^{3}=(a+b)({a}^{2}-ab+{b}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write our expression as a sum of cubes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{27}+{z}^{3}={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3}+{z}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the sum of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{27}+{z}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3}+{z}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}+z)({(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot z+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}+z)(\\frac{1}{9}-\\frac{z}{3}+{z}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b77b7c3f-41cf-5d84-b905-19ea7c30c507"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b77b7c3f-41cf-5d84-b905-19ea7c30c507" :answer-field/id #uuid "b77b7c3f-41cf-5d84-b905-19ea7c30c507" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f1b9f62d-cf08-5a64-aeb7-1cab2092352e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-62a351de-d274-575d-b200-5387096347ba" "answer-f1b9f62d-cf08-5a64-aeb7-1cab2092352e" "answer-bb2085ff-c8a6-5372-acca-6f59e8fca692" "answer-3def5748-f5f8-53b5-b6ea-8e14945dfed5" "answer-93a145b7-2cd7-59b3-8ff3-26e918720c2a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62a351de-d274-575d-b200-5387096347ba" :answer/id #uuid "62a351de-d274-575d-b200-5387096347ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3}+z)(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{z}{2}+{z}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1b9f62d-cf08-5a64-aeb7-1cab2092352e" :answer/id #uuid "f1b9f62d-cf08-5a64-aeb7-1cab2092352e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3}+z)(\\frac{1}{9}-\\frac{z}{3}+{z}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb2085ff-c8a6-5372-acca-6f59e8fca692" :answer/id #uuid "bb2085ff-c8a6-5372-acca-6f59e8fca692" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3}-z)(\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{z}{3}+{z}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3def5748-f5f8-53b5-b6ea-8e14945dfed5" :answer/id #uuid "3def5748-f5f8-53b5-b6ea-8e14945dfed5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{9}+z)(\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{z}{9}+{z}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93a145b7-2cd7-59b3-8ff3-26e918720c2a" :answer/id #uuid "93a145b7-2cd7-59b3-8ff3-26e918720c2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{9}-z)(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{z}{9}+{z}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068745 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-faeaf850-1aff-5e10-9fc0-51026df9825a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-faeaf850-1aff-5e10-9fc0-51026df9825a" :question/id #uuid "faeaf850-1aff-5e10-9fc0-51026df9825a" :question/math-academy-id "q-37550" :question/problem "Factor the expression $8{x}^{6}+27{y}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "The sum of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}+{b}^{3}=(a+b)({a}^{2}-ab+{b}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write our expression as a sum of cubes, as follows:\n\n$$\n8{x}^{6}+27{y}^{3}={(2{x}^{2})}^{3}+(3y)^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the sum of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{6}+27{y}^{3} & ={(2{x}^{2})}^{3}+(3y)^{3} \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2}+3y)({(2{x}^{2})}^{2}-2{x}^{2}\\cdot 3y+(3y)^{2}) \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2}+3y)(4{x}^{4}-6{x}^{2}y+9{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-60bed6b5-bec1-5f6b-a052-99e12b1d3687"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-60bed6b5-bec1-5f6b-a052-99e12b1d3687" :answer-field/id #uuid "60bed6b5-bec1-5f6b-a052-99e12b1d3687" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-66384dc3-aeed-50fd-a06a-ddf8c266eef1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f80932c6-3e4d-5cf6-8c1e-db1595d5dff2" "answer-89f89f32-bf9b-503e-9126-2f6e0c9a76d0" "answer-dcec1f73-9359-594e-a38c-ef8b08ea1fca" "answer-66384dc3-aeed-50fd-a06a-ddf8c266eef1" "answer-a7d05e8a-af35-596b-816d-9f0b78413663"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f80932c6-3e4d-5cf6-8c1e-db1595d5dff2" :answer/id #uuid "f80932c6-3e4d-5cf6-8c1e-db1595d5dff2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2x+3y)(2{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89f89f32-bf9b-503e-9126-2f6e0c9a76d0" :answer/id #uuid "89f89f32-bf9b-503e-9126-2f6e0c9a76d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2x+3y)(4{x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}y+3{y}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcec1f73-9359-594e-a38c-ef8b08ea1fca" :answer/id #uuid "dcec1f73-9359-594e-a38c-ef8b08ea1fca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2{x}^{2}+3y)(2{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}y+3{y}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-66384dc3-aeed-50fd-a06a-ddf8c266eef1" :answer/id #uuid "66384dc3-aeed-50fd-a06a-ddf8c266eef1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2{x}^{2}+3y)(4{x}^{4}-6{x}^{2}y+9{y}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7d05e8a-af35-596b-816d-9f0b78413663" :answer/id #uuid "a7d05e8a-af35-596b-816d-9f0b78413663" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2{x}^{2}-3y)(4{x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}y+9{y}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-82176286-a684-5858-a421-cc4a4b4187bd" :question/id #uuid "82176286-a684-5858-a421-cc4a4b4187bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-69640" :question/problem "Factor $\\frac{8}{{t}^{6}}-1000.$" :question/worked-solution "The difference of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}-{b}^{3}=(a-b)({a}^{2}+ab+{b}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNotice that all of the terms have a common factor of $8.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $8,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{{t}^{6}}-1000=8(\\frac{1}{{t}^{6}}-125)\n$$\n\nThe expression in parentheses can be written as a difference of cubes:\n\n$$\n8(\\frac{1}{{t}^{6}}-125)=8({(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})}^{3}-{5}^{3})\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression in parentheses using the difference of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{{t}^{6}}-1000 & =8({(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})}^{3}-{5}^{3}) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-5)({(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot 5+{5}^{2}) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-5)(\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}+\\frac{5}{{t}^{2}}+25)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7b298728-bbf2-5c29-9156-fbe40121077d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7b298728-bbf2-5c29-9156-fbe40121077d" :answer-field/id #uuid "7b298728-bbf2-5c29-9156-fbe40121077d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-06a88805-1323-5ff5-8f48-42543b26f532" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4903b99d-0682-5a79-8fb4-e2b4b7c0f41f" "answer-50b5e943-9ca0-5aed-9175-6b9302edbbc9" "answer-42f98072-159d-5073-bea3-fc772168038c" "answer-06a88805-1323-5ff5-8f48-42543b26f532" "answer-ee53056d-d85d-50b2-b983-34612d0e6c4b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4903b99d-0682-5a79-8fb4-e2b4b7c0f41f" :answer/id #uuid "4903b99d-0682-5a79-8fb4-e2b4b7c0f41f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8(\\frac{1}{t}+5)(\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}-\\frac{5}{{t}^{2}}+25)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50b5e943-9ca0-5aed-9175-6b9302edbbc9" :answer/id #uuid "50b5e943-9ca0-5aed-9175-6b9302edbbc9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8(\\frac{1}{t}-5)(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42f98072-159d-5073-bea3-fc772168038c" :answer/id #uuid "42f98072-159d-5073-bea3-fc772168038c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+5)(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06a88805-1323-5ff5-8f48-42543b26f532" :answer/id #uuid "06a88805-1323-5ff5-8f48-42543b26f532" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-5)(\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}+\\frac{5}{{t}^{2}}+25)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee53056d-d85d-50b2-b983-34612d0e6c4b" :answer/id #uuid "ee53056d-d85d-50b2-b983-34612d0e6c4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-5)(\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-cd2e9b70-6a0e-52a0-8281-13d7ee6987ee" :activity/id #uuid "cd2e9b70-6a0e-52a0-8281-13d7ee6987ee" :activity/title "Factoring Sums and Differences of Cubes" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049153 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-fc929762-2aa0-5d2e-bd54-0dbd988def7b" "step-e10bbf89-2bab-5c2a-9845-b34b37adb348" "step-327cbab5-20c5-52bc-9726-24f78d338f9d"] :activity/first-step "step-fc929762-2aa0-5d2e-bd54-0dbd988def7b" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-fc929762-2aa0-5d2e-bd54-0dbd988def7b" :step/id #uuid "fc929762-2aa0-5d2e-bd54-0dbd988def7b" :step/content "question-733ab756-eb23-5365-83ae-dfce071139ee" :step/next "step-e10bbf89-2bab-5c2a-9845-b34b37adb348" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e10bbf89-2bab-5c2a-9845-b34b37adb348" :step/id #uuid "e10bbf89-2bab-5c2a-9845-b34b37adb348" :step/content "question-faeaf850-1aff-5e10-9fc0-51026df9825a" :step/next "step-327cbab5-20c5-52bc-9726-24f78d338f9d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-327cbab5-20c5-52bc-9726-24f78d338f9d" :step/id #uuid "327cbab5-20c5-52bc-9726-24f78d338f9d" :step/content "question-82176286-a684-5858-a421-cc4a4b4187bd" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204107 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d96fd40c-87e8-5ffa-bd33-b4b724c33445"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204604 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}5.711 & 3.866 & -3.400 \\\\ 3.580 & -0.216 & -3.789 \\\\ -0.507 & -5.693 & -3.727 \\\\ 1.640 & -0.269 & 0.705 \\\\ -0.225 & -4.111 & -2.130\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe wish to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset while still preserving as much variance as possible.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning that walks through the process of reducing the dimensionality of the dataset.\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. The first principal component is \n{{field-1}} \nwith the \n{{field-2}} \nvariance.\n\nThe second principal component is the direction in feature space \n{{field-3}} \nto the first\n{{field-4}}. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components, picking the number of components based on the amount of cumulative variance preserved.\n\nThe first step is to {{field-5}}.\n\nThe standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{std}}=[\\begin{aligned}1.40 & 1.37 & -0.49 \\\\ 0.59 & 0.29 & -0.70 \\\\ -0.97 & -1.18 & -0.66 \\\\ -0.15 & 0.27 & 1.67 \\\\ -0.86 & -0.75 & 0.18\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe SVD of the standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{\\text{std}} & =UΣ{V}^{T},\\,\\text{where} \\\\ U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.713 & 0.123 & -0.47 & 0.506 & 0.013 \\\\ -0.25 & 0.321 & 0.709 & 0.213 & 0.535 \\\\ 0.509 & 0.422 & 0.046 & 0.667 & -0.342 \\\\ 0.042 & -0.839 & 0.241 & 0.487 & 0.01 \\\\ 0.41 & -0.027 & -0.465 & 0.133 & 0.773\\end{aligned}], \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.809 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.020 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.114 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}], \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.711 & 0.051 & 0.701 \\\\ -0.693 & -0.218 & -0.687 \\\\ 0.118 & -0.975 & 0.191\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\nThe fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n{{field-6}}.\n\nThe fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n{{field-7}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-8}}.\n\nWriting ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ for the columns of $V$ and ${u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3},{u}_{4},{u}_{5}$ for the columns of $U$, the reduced dataset is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{red}}={{field-9}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. The first principal component is $\\text{the direction in feature space}$ with the $\\text{most}$ variance.\n\nThe second principal component is the direction $\\text{orthogonal}$ to the first $\\text{principal component}.$\n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components, picking the number of components based on the amount of cumulative variance preserved.\n\nThe first step is to $\\text{standardize the data}.$\n\nThe standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{std}}=[\\begin{aligned}1.40 & 1.37 & -0.49 \\\\ 0.59 & 0.29 & -0.70 \\\\ -0.97 & -1.18 & -0.66 \\\\ -0.15 & 0.27 & 1.67 \\\\ -0.86 & -0.75 & 0.18\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe SVD of the standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{\\text{std}} & =UΣ{V}^{T},\\,\\text{where} \\\\ U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.713 & 0.123 & -0.47 & 0.506 & 0.013 \\\\ -0.25 & 0.321 & 0.709 & 0.213 & 0.535 \\\\ 0.509 & 0.422 & 0.046 & 0.667 & -0.342 \\\\ 0.042 & -0.839 & 0.241 & 0.487 & 0.01 \\\\ 0.41 & -0.027 & -0.465 & 0.133 & 0.773\\end{aligned}], \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.809 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.020 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.114 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}], \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.711 & 0.051 & 0.701 \\\\ -0.693 & -0.218 & -0.687 \\\\ 0.118 & -0.975 & 0.191\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{2.809}^{2}}{{2.809}^{2}+{2.020}^{2}+{0.114}^{2}}≈65.8%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{2.809}^{2}+{2.020}^{2}}{{2.809}^{2}+{2.020}^{2}+{0.114}^{2}}≈99.9%.\n$$\n\nThus, we have that \n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $65.8%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $99.9%$ of the variance.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions}$ , since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90%$ of the variance.\n\nWriting ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ for the columns of $V$ and ${u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3},{u}_{4},{u}_{5}$ for the columns of $U$, the reduced dataset is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{red}}={X}_{\\text{std}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & {v}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nsince we need to project the data onto the first two principal components, which are the first two columns of $V.$\n\n**Note:** In order to invert the projection and interpret any further analysis in terms of the original features, we need to know the means and standard deviations of the original dataset $X.$ Additionally, in case we have more data, we can use the means and standard deviations of the original dataset to map the new data into the original feature space." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a6a2fc9a-fa60-5b92-bb1f-83a6ff5a87fc" "answer-field-8f878aa0-5ae9-5f8d-ba8b-cf856c2ae09b" "answer-field-41e8432a-796f-5670-a40a-ab87f8a7dfda" "answer-field-37c49f27-91d3-57d4-89