:db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11f202f0-5a2c-50a9-86a4-8e1f90d2fc6c" :answer/id #uuid "11f202f0-5a2c-50a9-86a4-8e1f90d2fc6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3aba9863-6882-5c55-b998-3acd603f7eb2" :answer/id #uuid "3aba9863-6882-5c55-b998-3acd603f7eb2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b071d322-d430-5f91-b157-0d44ee6fac29" :question/id #uuid "b071d322-d430-5f91-b157-0d44ee6fac29" :question/math-academy-id "q-100019" :question/problem "$7{log}_{2}⁡t=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡({c}^{m})=m\\operatorname{log}⁡(c)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n7{log}_{2}⁡t={log}_{2}⁡({t}^{7}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eba2b04a-58b1-5797-a2dc-057b2fcfd9dd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eba2b04a-58b1-5797-a2dc-057b2fcfd9dd" :answer-field/id #uuid "eba2b04a-58b1-5797-a2dc-057b2fcfd9dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f7a71173-deae-5155-8154-2b2bf6b3687d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-312ee280-346c-51be-9a5b-40f3ad5f100e" "answer-61eaa231-d53f-5412-a297-547a805089db" "answer-5543e5e9-fa34-55a2-963c-46317805891c" "answer-ce45adcc-4013-5eaa-828b-6c4c5f9aefd3" "answer-f7a71173-deae-5155-8154-2b2bf6b3687d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-312ee280-346c-51be-9a5b-40f3ad5f100e" :answer/id #uuid "312ee280-346c-51be-9a5b-40f3ad5f100e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(7t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61eaa231-d53f-5412-a297-547a805089db" :answer/id #uuid "61eaa231-d53f-5412-a297-547a805089db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{7t}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5543e5e9-fa34-55a2-963c-46317805891c" :answer/id #uuid "5543e5e9-fa34-55a2-963c-46317805891c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{7}}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce45adcc-4013-5eaa-828b-6c4c5f9aefd3" :answer/id #uuid "ce45adcc-4013-5eaa-828b-6c4c5f9aefd3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(t+7)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7a71173-deae-5155-8154-2b2bf6b3687d" :answer/id #uuid "f7a71173-deae-5155-8154-2b2bf6b3687d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡({t}^{7})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-03b07b19-1587-5e10-a223-7c9ddb411106" :question/id #uuid "03b07b19-1587-5e10-a223-7c9ddb411106" :question/math-academy-id "q-25145" :question/problem "Find the value of ${log}_{z}⁡(512)$ if ${log}_{z}⁡(8)=0.3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms and the information given, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{z}⁡(512) & ={log}_{z}⁡({8}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {log}_{z}⁡(8) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-75dda720-e013-520a-938a-10e65394a073"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-75dda720-e013-520a-938a-10e65394a073" :answer-field/id #uuid "75dda720-e013-520a-938a-10e65394a073" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-31562391-7c74-5890-90fa-889e3aa34e17" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9d89af4f-a4fa-57ad-9a60-eeefd780e3d8" "answer-c2891e28-3997-5c59-b029-0adbbbea8559" "answer-31562391-7c74-5890-90fa-889e3aa34e17" "answer-e4bcee11-548a-590b-b15f-c15919dd5066" "answer-93690d65-87ee-5029-83ee-8aa7788d28de"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d89af4f-a4fa-57ad-9a60-eeefd780e3d8" :answer/id #uuid "9d89af4f-a4fa-57ad-9a60-eeefd780e3d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2891e28-3997-5c59-b029-0adbbbea8559" :answer/id #uuid "c2891e28-3997-5c59-b029-0adbbbea8559" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31562391-7c74-5890-90fa-889e3aa34e17" :answer/id #uuid "31562391-7c74-5890-90fa-889e3aa34e17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4bcee11-548a-590b-b15f-c15919dd5066" :answer/id #uuid "e4bcee11-548a-590b-b15f-c15919dd5066" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93690d65-87ee-5029-83ee-8aa7788d28de" :answer/id #uuid "93690d65-87ee-5029-83ee-8aa7788d28de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-abb937a1-e6d0-5e25-8254-46eecc862782" :question/id #uuid "abb937a1-e6d0-5e25-8254-46eecc862782" :question/math-academy-id "q-100023" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)=5,$ what is $\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{6})?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡({c}^{m})=m\\operatorname{log}⁡(c)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms and the information given, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{6}) & =6\\cdot \\operatorname{log}⁡(x) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-73a7d0d1-417d-5e12-a193-f77620a98d40"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-73a7d0d1-417d-5e12-a193-f77620a98d40" :answer-field/id #uuid "73a7d0d1-417d-5e12-a193-f77620a98d40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-89eb1903-3acb-5d99-ada8-b7adb94e0a37" :answer-field/choices ["answer-38b09c14-4584-5a4a-8fab-a6afa59d05d9" "answer-769ba4be-c9e6-5ea8-8442-531a20987224" "answer-89eb1903-3acb-5d99-ada8-b7adb94e0a37" "answer-6611e271-528b-5265-b8d2-31b7bc0ef131" "answer-b337f1d4-9e63-591c-b7d2-ab05504f9d5e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38b09c14-4584-5a4a-8fab-a6afa59d05d9" :answer/id #uuid "38b09c14-4584-5a4a-8fab-a6afa59d05d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-769ba4be-c9e6-5ea8-8442-531a20987224" :answer/id #uuid "769ba4be-c9e6-5ea8-8442-531a20987224" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "125" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89eb1903-3acb-5d99-ada8-b7adb94e0a37" :answer/id #uuid "89eb1903-3acb-5d99-ada8-b7adb94e0a37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6611e271-528b-5265-b8d2-31b7bc0ef131" :answer/id #uuid "6611e271-528b-5265-b8d2-31b7bc0ef131" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "300" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b337f1d4-9e63-591c-b7d2-ab05504f9d5e" :answer/id #uuid "b337f1d4-9e63-591c-b7d2-ab05504f9d5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "625" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111880 :tutorial/content "The **power rule** allows us to simplify logarithms of exponentiated expressions. It states that\n\n$$\n\\log_{n}\\left( c^{\\color{blue}m} \\right) = {\\color{blue}m}\\log_{n}{c}.\n$$\n\nIn other words, the logarithm has the effect of \"bringing down the power.\"\n\nFor example, let's use the rule to simplify $\\log\\left(125\\right)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(125) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({5}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To understand why the power rule works, notice that we can reach the same result using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(125) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({5}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{log}⁡(5) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power rule is just a shortcut for the above procedure." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110844 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186107763 :answer-field/choices 17592186107764] [:db/retract 17592186107763 :answer-field/choices 17592186107765] [:db/retract 17592186107763 :answer-field/choices 17592186107766] [:db/retract 17592186107763 :answer-field/choices 17592186107767] [:db/retract 17592186108617 :answer-field/choices 17592186108618] [:db/retract 17592186108617 :answer-field/choices 17592186108619] [:db/retract 17592186108617 :answer-field/choices 17592186108620] [:db/retract 17592186108617 :answer-field/choices 17592186108621] [:db/retract 17592186108617 :answer-field/choices 17592186108622] {:db/id 17592186110682 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f95ce679-5df8-536b-9a6e-bc83e05e288c" "question-81da0bad-95e8-53a0-9f19-ddc55b628c5f" "question-5c63aa2a-bac5-5697-a40d-14aec41163e5" "question-eb877a39-4dd4-5455-8e16-f850f269ea03" "question-4a638344-d920-5adc-900d-8c273723b689"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110462 :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\sqrt{3}\\sin{\\theta }\\tan{\\theta }-\\sin{\\theta } = 0$ for $0^\\circ \\leq \\theta <  360^\\circ.$ If the solutions $\\theta_1, \\theta_2, \\theta_3, \\theta_4$ are written in ascending order, then what is the numerical value of $\\theta_2\\cdot \\theta_3?$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the function $\\tan{\\theta}$ is not defined at $\\theta = 90^\\circ , 270^\\circ,$ so these values cannot be solutions to the equation. \n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\sin{\\theta } = 0, \\qquad \\sqrt{3}\\tan{\\theta } - 1 =0.\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sin{\\theta} = 0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq \\theta <  360^\\circ$  are $\\theta = 0^\\circ, \\, 180^\\circ.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\sqrt{3}\\tan{\\theta } - 1 =0,$ or equivalently $\\tan{\\theta} =  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$ The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq \\theta <  360^\\circ$ are $\\theta=30^\\circ, \\, 210^\\circ.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = 0^\\circ, \\quad \\theta_2= 30^\\circ, \\quad \\theta_3=180^\\circ, \\quad \\theta_4= 210^\\circ.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\theta_2\\cdot \\theta_3 =30 \\cdot  180 = 5\\,400.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110683 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-df0bb882-2469-5cab-82f5-df4eee26a54c" "question-49fd94b7-d87e-5482-b74a-14a44acf4a47" "question-df31d07c-21a6-58c5-a19d-926033829f92" "question-0135fc07-6118-52f5-9b57-2dcf9eca07ac"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110461 :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\sqrt{2} \\sin{x} \\cos{x} = -\\sin{x}$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq \\pi.$ If the solutions $x_1, x_2, x_3$ are written in ascending order, then what is $x_2\\cdot x_3?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = 0, \\qquad \\sqrt{2} \\cos{x} + 1 =0.\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sin{x} = 0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\leq x \\leq \\pi$  are $x = 0,\\, \\pi.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\sqrt{2} \\cos{x} + 1= 0,$ or equivalently $\\cos{x} =-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$ The solution to this equation for $0\\leq x \\leq \\pi$  is $x =\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0,\\quad x_2=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4},\\quad x_3= \\pi.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nx_2\\cdot x_3 = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\cdot  \\pi=\\dfrac{3\\pi^2}{4}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110684 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a135a2c5-e84d-52d5-89de-19fd76614f68" "question-68f4987d-ed26-5738-89d9-828f518f37d4" "question-fc1ed235-5329-5107-b309-feec267a8cde" "question-cf8eafa9-ba3d-54d7-ae84-18acaa32c81c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110463 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\csc{x} \\tan{x} = \\tan{x}$ for $0^\\circ \\leq x <  360^\\circ.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the function $\\csc{x}$ is not defined at the points where $\\sin{x} = 0.$ On the interval $0^\\circ \\leq x <  360^\\circ,$ the excluded values are $x = 0^\\circ , 180^\\circ.$\n\nOn the other hand, the function $\\tan{x}$ is not defined at the points where $\\cos{x} = 0.$ In the given interval, the excluded values are $x = 90^\\circ , 270^\\circ.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-1)\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\csc{x} - 1 = 0, \\qquad \\tan{x} = 0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\csc{x} -1= 0,$ or equivalently, $\\csc x=1.$ The solution to this equation for $0 ^\\circ \\leq x \\lt 360^\\circ$ is $x = 90^\\circ.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\tan{x} = 0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ\\leq x \\lt 360^\\circ$  are $x = 0^\\circ,\\, 180^\\circ.$\n\nFinally, taking into account the values excluded from the interval, we conclude that the given equation has no solution." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110685 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-22a4714b-c9b9-595d-bd56-cdf122336677" "question-aa82973f-2912-57c5-acdd-b4b8b9abbe44"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110464 :question/problem "The graph shows the curve $y = 2\\sin{x}\\cos{x}$ and the curve $y = \\cos{x}$ on the interval $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ.$ The curves intersect at the points $A,$ $B,$ $C,$ and $D,$ as shown. Find $x$-coordinates of these points.\n\n![](images/d3/d3462ebb123dcaf59391787ffae5b39dea5809eefe656cfda2f17498259dc1b6.png)" :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$ values of both graphs are the same. This gives \nthe equation\n\n$$\n2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\cos{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\cos{x}  = 0, \\qquad 2\\sin{x} - 1 = 0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\cos{x} = 0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$ are $x= 90^\\circ, \\, 270^\\circ.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\sin{x} - 1  = 0,$ or equivalently $\\sin{x}=\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$ are $x = 30^\\circ,\\, 150^\\circ.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\nx_1=30^\\circ, \\quad x_2=90^\\circ,\\quad x_3=150^\\circ, \\quad x_4 = 270^\\circ.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-f95ce679-5df8-536b-9a6e-bc83e05e288c" :question/id #uuid "f95ce679-5df8-536b-9a6e-bc83e05e288c" :question/math-academy-id "q-23996" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2)=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "By the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x\\le {180}^{∘}$  are $x={0}^{∘},{180}^{∘}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2.$ But since $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\le 1,$ then $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2$ has no solution.\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={0}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0\\cdot 180=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-853b9a45-07a0-5d79-aac9-5ddaf7fb8d59"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-853b9a45-07a0-5d79-aac9-5ddaf7fb8d59" :answer-field/id #uuid "853b9a45-07a0-5d79-aac9-5ddaf7fb8d59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-46f53105-09bf-502b-b472-e933fae8750c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-46f53105-09bf-502b-b472-e933fae8750c" "answer-13d379b4-fd86-5552-9424-edc289fe4370" "answer-3d0c1c93-dac8-5aff-a40b-4d6ff43c11c5" "answer-b029ed80-929b-58d1-85a6-c1480f689058" "answer-3c5a0acb-466f-54ae-8690-a2b138fe3d18"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46f53105-09bf-502b-b472-e933fae8750c" :answer/id #uuid "46f53105-09bf-502b-b472-e933fae8750c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13d379b4-fd86-5552-9424-edc289fe4370" :answer/id #uuid "13d379b4-fd86-5552-9424-edc289fe4370" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "120" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d0c1c93-dac8-5aff-a40b-4d6ff43c11c5" :answer/id #uuid "3d0c1c93-dac8-5aff-a40b-4d6ff43c11c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "180" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b029ed80-929b-58d1-85a6-c1480f689058" :answer/id #uuid "b029ed80-929b-58d1-85a6-c1480f689058" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "240" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c5a0acb-466f-54ae-8690-a2b138fe3d18" :answer/id #uuid "3c5a0acb-466f-54ae-8690-a2b138fe3d18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "90" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-81da0bad-95e8-53a0-9f19-ddc55b628c5f" :question/id #uuid "81da0bad-95e8-53a0-9f19-ddc55b628c5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-338464" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the function $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is not defined at $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={120}^{∘},\\,{300}^{∘}.$\n\nTaking into account that ${90}^{∘}$ and ${270}^{∘}$ are excluded values, and arranging the remaining solutions in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={120}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={300}^{∘}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8c6598f4-0fbf-50af-884c-720c85efe7f8" "answer-field-2005716b-94ca-58b9-b1e8-6bc420209440"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8c6598f4-0fbf-50af-884c-720c85efe7f8" :answer-field/id #uuid "8c6598f4-0fbf-50af-884c-720c85efe7f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b04751f9-d84f-529e-9019-27752cc3a07a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b04751f9-d84f-529e-9019-27752cc3a07a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b04751f9-d84f-529e-9019-27752cc3a07a" :answer/id #uuid "b04751f9-d84f-529e-9019-27752cc3a07a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "120" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2005716b-94ca-58b9-b1e8-6bc420209440" :answer-field/id #uuid "2005716b-94ca-58b9-b1e8-6bc420209440" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-caa5f594-8176-5c26-b7e8-5e8be95850cc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-caa5f594-8176-5c26-b7e8-5e8be95850cc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-caa5f594-8176-5c26-b7e8-5e8be95850cc" :answer/id #uuid "caa5f594-8176-5c26-b7e8-5e8be95850cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "300" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5c63aa2a-bac5-5697-a40d-14aec41163e5" :question/id #uuid "5c63aa2a-bac5-5697-a40d-14aec41163e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-24178" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the function $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is not defined at $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$ \n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+3=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$  are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+3=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={120}^{∘},\\,{300}^{∘}.$\n\nTaking into account that ${90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}$ are excluded values, and combining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={120}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={300}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=120\\cdot 300=36\\,000.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bfaff15c-8394-5bdb-8554-c3a83444ae51"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bfaff15c-8394-5bdb-8554-c3a83444ae51" :answer-field/id #uuid "bfaff15c-8394-5bdb-8554-c3a83444ae51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3bfe7f99-5d4f-5ab8-8bd3-cc14bb9336f9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2536465b-5ecd-5518-9be7-2990d421c9fa" "answer-5492dbb1-a688-58a2-8864-d6418a74cf80" "answer-3bfe7f99-5d4f-5ab8-8bd3-cc14bb9336f9" "answer-783b0db3-0d5a-52d0-8afa-ca071f9c1bff" "answer-cc4f4608-c3d1-5144-888e-f41391f17a0a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2536465b-5ecd-5518-9be7-2990d421c9fa" :answer/id #uuid "2536465b-5ecd-5518-9be7-2990d421c9fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24\\,300" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5492dbb1-a688-58a2-8864-d6418a74cf80" :answer/id #uuid "5492dbb1-a688-58a2-8864-d6418a74cf80" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32\\,400" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bfe7f99-5d4f-5ab8-8bd3-cc14bb9336f9" :answer/id #uuid "3bfe7f99-5d4f-5ab8-8bd3-cc14bb9336f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-783b0db3-0d5a-52d0-8afa-ca071f9c1bff" :answer/id #uuid "783b0db3-0d5a-52d0-8afa-ca071f9c1bff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\,400" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc4f4608-c3d1-5144-888e-f41391f17a0a" :answer/id #uuid "cc4f4608-c3d1-5144-888e-f41391f17a0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "81\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-eb877a39-4dd4-5455-8e16-f850f269ea03" :question/id #uuid "eb877a39-4dd4-5455-8e16-f850f269ea03" :question/math-academy-id "q-274637" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}=$ \n{{field-3}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}=$ \n{{field-4}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the function $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is not defined at $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$ \n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$  are $x={0}^{∘},\\,{180}^{∘}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={60}^{∘},\\,{240}^{∘}.$\n\nArranging all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={0}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={60}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}={180}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}={240}^{∘}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ac88126a-a1b8-5cf7-b0a7-3795a414dd95" "answer-field-93bfd4ba-3aa8-51f4-8ff1-3f2493c0f34d" "answer-field-5de29ce3-b3ef-56b1-91e3-39478ee6339a" "answer-field-e528e5fe-c376-5cb2-8628-5f10950784ca"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ac88126a-a1b8-5cf7-b0a7-3795a414dd95" :answer-field/id #uuid "ac88126a-a1b8-5cf7-b0a7-3795a414dd95" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ef319902-264e-5bc4-8699-a3a413d4add8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ef319902-264e-5bc4-8699-a3a413d4add8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef319902-264e-5bc4-8699-a3a413d4add8" :answer/id #uuid "ef319902-264e-5bc4-8699-a3a413d4add8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-93bfd4ba-3aa8-51f4-8ff1-3f2493c0f34d" :answer-field/id #uuid "93bfd4ba-3aa8-51f4-8ff1-3f2493c0f34d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4278b12b-558c-5213-b12e-e1f931274ffd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4278b12b-558c-5213-b12e-e1f931274ffd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4278b12b-558c-5213-b12e-e1f931274ffd" :answer/id #uuid "4278b12b-558c-5213-b12e-e1f931274ffd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "60" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5de29ce3-b3ef-56b1-91e3-39478ee6339a" :answer-field/id #uuid "5de29ce3-b3ef-56b1-91e3-39478ee6339a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a7e6317a-c800-5e65-832d-95b3f9a2062b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a7e6317a-c800-5e65-832d-95b3f9a2062b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7e6317a-c800-5e65-832d-95b3f9a2062b" :answer/id #uuid "a7e6317a-c800-5e65-832d-95b3f9a2062b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "180" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e528e5fe-c376-5cb2-8628-5f10950784ca" :answer-field/id #uuid "e528e5fe-c376-5cb2-8628-5f10950784ca" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9ecb7168-3658-502f-b92e-e5f8ec58c6a9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9ecb7168-3658-502f-b92e-e5f8ec58c6a9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ecb7168-3658-502f-b92e-e5f8ec58c6a9" :answer/id #uuid "9ecb7168-3658-502f-b92e-e5f8ec58c6a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "240" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4a638344-d920-5adc-900d-8c273723b689" :question/id #uuid "4a638344-d920-5adc-900d-8c273723b689" :question/math-academy-id "q-23987" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1)=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x\\le {180}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are written in ascending order, then what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "By the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ The solution to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x\\le {180}^{∘}$  is $x={90}^{∘}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x\\le {180}^{∘}$ are $x={30}^{∘},{150}^{∘}.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={30}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={90}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}={150}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=30\\cdot 150=4\\,500.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-337bac18-a16d-5942-8b9d-bc00008b3b4a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-337bac18-a16d-5942-8b9d-bc00008b3b4a" :answer-field/id #uuid "337bac18-a16d-5942-8b9d-bc00008b3b4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-32930bd5-ee8e-5a03-8ebb-b6b921877137" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cb250ce7-63ea-5a3c-8019-6b5aba0c0efd" "answer-6a1ff96a-cf38-5ddb-9d6e-419a210d9af7" "answer-80900602-f3a4-5944-ae40-68a14b46ef39" "answer-32930bd5-ee8e-5a03-8ebb-b6b921877137" "answer-9ef7b925-46c5-59ff-8710-15af50538fa0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb250ce7-63ea-5a3c-8019-6b5aba0c0efd" :answer/id #uuid "cb250ce7-63ea-5a3c-8019-6b5aba0c0efd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13\\,500" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a1ff96a-cf38-5ddb-9d6e-419a210d9af7" :answer/id #uuid "6a1ff96a-cf38-5ddb-9d6e-419a210d9af7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "27\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80900602-f3a4-5944-ae40-68a14b46ef39" :answer/id #uuid "80900602-f3a4-5944-ae40-68a14b46ef39" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,700" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32930bd5-ee8e-5a03-8ebb-b6b921877137" :answer/id #uuid "32930bd5-ee8e-5a03-8ebb-b6b921877137" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,500" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ef7b925-46c5-59ff-8710-15af50538fa0" :answer/id #uuid "9ef7b925-46c5-59ff-8710-15af50538fa0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "900" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-df0bb882-2469-5cab-82f5-df4eee26a54c" :question/id #uuid "df0bb882-2469-5cab-82f5-df4eee26a54c" :question/math-academy-id "q-337172" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,{x}_{3}=$ \n{{field-3}}$,\\,{x}_{4}=$\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Note that the function $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ In the given interval, the excluded values are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=-\\pi ,\\,0.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTaking into account that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are excluded values, and arranging all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\pi ,\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,{x}_{3}=0,\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c0bff5ee-f0bb-5564-95ca-9d1ccf76e9fb" "answer-field-d0b6ab9f-590f-55f8-9d84-51450b9afb62" "answer-field-6a28f71e-2de4-57c1-adc0-73f5c09d376a" "answer-field-ec791608-10e6-5073-b263-73fdaf240d93"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c0bff5ee-f0bb-5564-95ca-9d1ccf76e9fb" :answer-field/id #uuid "c0bff5ee-f0bb-5564-95ca-9d1ccf76e9fb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-51727bef-15f4-56e8-b56b-7273e05fb720" :answer-field/choices ["answer-51727bef-15f4-56e8-b56b-7273e05fb720"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51727bef-15f4-56e8-b56b-7273e05fb720" :answer/id #uuid "51727bef-15f4-56e8-b56b-7273e05fb720" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d0b6ab9f-590f-55f8-9d84-51450b9afb62" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0b6ab9f-590f-55f8-9d84-51450b9afb62" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2a164d99-c5d0-5aca-9c2d-79ffdfb6a7ac" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2a164d99-c5d0-5aca-9c2d-79ffdfb6a7ac"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a164d99-c5d0-5aca-9c2d-79ffdfb6a7ac" :answer/id #uuid "2a164d99-c5d0-5aca-9c2d-79ffdfb6a7ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6a28f71e-2de4-57c1-adc0-73f5c09d376a" :answer-field/id #uuid "6a28f71e-2de4-57c1-adc0-73f5c09d376a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a6769fc2-1f99-58cd-b304-39fd1bc85cf7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a6769fc2-1f99-58cd-b304-39fd1bc85cf7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6769fc2-1f99-58cd-b304-39fd1bc85cf7" :answer/id #uuid "a6769fc2-1f99-58cd-b304-39fd1bc85cf7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ec791608-10e6-5073-b263-73fdaf240d93" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec791608-10e6-5073-b263-73fdaf240d93" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a7dfe15a-21a9-5c24-bd6e-8f28beddda63" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a7dfe15a-21a9-5c24-bd6e-8f28beddda63"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7dfe15a-21a9-5c24-bd6e-8f28beddda63" :answer/id #uuid "a7dfe15a-21a9-5c24-bd6e-8f28beddda63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-49fd94b7-d87e-5482-b74a-14a44acf4a47" :question/id #uuid "49fd94b7-d87e-5482-b74a-14a44acf4a47" :question/math-academy-id "q-337256" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}$\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}$\\,{x}_{3}=$ \n{{field-3}}$\\,{x}_{4}=$ \n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=-\\pi ,\\,0.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nArranging all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\pi ,\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,{x}_{3}=0,\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-be519b0a-648a-5300-b22a-fd5381ebda77" "answer-field-76c89bcd-2113-5515-8496-363099c12112" "answer-field-0e3a0c55-cd01-5d33-8da4-0ce82f945812" "answer-field-042639be-7137-5f5e-a238-735f62cbd938"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-be519b0a-648a-5300-b22a-fd5381ebda77" :answer-field/id #uuid "be519b0a-648a-5300-b22a-fd5381ebda77" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-80a76a26-ed69-5db7-9d6a-b9df332671a4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-80a76a26-ed69-5db7-9d6a-b9df332671a4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80a76a26-ed69-5db7-9d6a-b9df332671a4" :answer/id #uuid "80a76a26-ed69-5db7-9d6a-b9df332671a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-76c89bcd-2113-5515-8496-363099c12112" :answer-field/id #uuid "76c89bcd-2113-5515-8496-363099c12112" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ff41c5fe-01c7-50f0-b67f-e728c1c6baaf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ff41c5fe-01c7-50f0-b67f-e728c1c6baaf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff41c5fe-01c7-50f0-b67f-e728c1c6baaf" :answer/id #uuid "ff41c5fe-01c7-50f0-b67f-e728c1c6baaf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0e3a0c55-cd01-5d33-8da4-0ce82f945812" :answer-field/id #uuid "0e3a0c55-cd01-5d33-8da4-0ce82f945812" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-72383d72-d843-5dec-a367-e09d7f144a46" :answer-field/choices ["answer-72383d72-d843-5dec-a367-e09d7f144a46"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72383d72-d843-5dec-a367-e09d7f144a46" :answer/id #uuid "72383d72-d843-5dec-a367-e09d7f144a46" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-042639be-7137-5f5e-a238-735f62cbd938" :answer-field/id #uuid "042639be-7137-5f5e-a238-735f62cbd938" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-233192cd-d748-5492-83f5-a8ca027c213a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-233192cd-d748-5492-83f5-a8ca027c213a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-233192cd-d748-5492-83f5-a8ca027c213a" :answer/id #uuid "233192cd-d748-5492-83f5-a8ca027c213a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111010 :question/problem "Consider the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, then what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $  are $x=-\\pi ,\\,0.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $  are $x=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\pi ,\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\,{x}_{3}=0,\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-\\pi )\\cdot (-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107763 :answer-field/correct "answer-3a612fb7-85ca-5e1e-a0d9-05385206ab45" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f842e257-c55e-5142-8502-c18e6a995408" "answer-3a612fb7-85ca-5e1e-a0d9-05385206ab45" "answer-b8fa603c-0bd7-5243-8435-dee5cbc797af" "answer-1ef3adf3-04fa-5f75-a8bf-08d06229dccb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f842e257-c55e-5142-8502-c18e6a995408" :answer/id #uuid "f842e257-c55e-5142-8502-c18e6a995408" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a612fb7-85ca-5e1e-a0d9-05385206ab45" :answer/id #uuid "3a612fb7-85ca-5e1e-a0d9-05385206ab45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8fa603c-0bd7-5243-8435-dee5cbc797af" :answer/id #uuid "b8fa603c-0bd7-5243-8435-dee5cbc797af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ef3adf3-04fa-5f75-a8bf-08d06229dccb" :answer/id #uuid "1ef3adf3-04fa-5f75-a8bf-08d06229dccb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-df31d07c-21a6-58c5-a19d-926033829f92" :question/id #uuid "df31d07c-21a6-58c5-a19d-926033829f92" :question/math-academy-id "q-337260" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}$\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Note that the function $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is not defined at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{7}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{7}=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=0,\\,\\pi .$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{7}=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{7}.$ But since $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\le 1,$ then $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{7}$ has no solution.\n\nArranging all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=0,\\,{x}_{2}=\\pi .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-74437d54-a301-5d58-b479-7b7e7c592eac" "answer-field-cb0ac175-522d-5c1a-bb1b-cfce425ebb5a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-74437d54-a301-5d58-b479-7b7e7c592eac" :answer-field/id #uuid "74437d54-a301-5d58-b479-7b7e7c592eac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-711256d5-4b36-5ac3-9517-2d23a59d4b59" :answer-field/choices ["answer-711256d5-4b36-5ac3-9517-2d23a59d4b59"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-711256d5-4b36-5ac3-9517-2d23a59d4b59" :answer/id #uuid "711256d5-4b36-5ac3-9517-2d23a59d4b59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cb0ac175-522d-5c1a-bb1b-cfce425ebb5a" :answer-field/id #uuid "cb0ac175-522d-5c1a-bb1b-cfce425ebb5a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5473a08d-0b43-5ff6-a9ad-c035a709b7a4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5473a08d-0b43-5ff6-a9ad-c035a709b7a4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5473a08d-0b43-5ff6-a9ad-c035a709b7a4" :answer/id #uuid "5473a08d-0b43-5ff6-a9ad-c035a709b7a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0135fc07-6118-52f5-9b57-2dcf9eca07ac" :question/id #uuid "0135fc07-6118-52f5-9b57-2dcf9eca07ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-337265" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}$\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{7}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{7}=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=0,\\,\\pi .$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{7}=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{7}.$ But since $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\le 1,$ then $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{7}$ has no solution.\n\nArranging all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=0,\\,{x}_{2}=\\pi .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a82f275d-a89c-55d9-9ccb-71905ddc863a" "answer-field-9dafbda0-33c7-55d7-95af-3b84510f7137"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a82f275d-a89c-55d9-9ccb-71905ddc863a" :answer-field/id #uuid "a82f275d-a89c-55d9-9ccb-71905ddc863a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4c9ed5c3-470f-5713-9a8f-70c76e807b4d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4c9ed5c3-470f-5713-9a8f-70c76e807b4d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c9ed5c3-470f-5713-9a8f-70c76e807b4d" :answer/id #uuid "4c9ed5c3-470f-5713-9a8f-70c76e807b4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9dafbda0-33c7-55d7-95af-3b84510f7137" :answer-field/id #uuid "9dafbda0-33c7-55d7-95af-3b84510f7137" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-12c635bd-4dab-5b49-9f10-53f8bdb5e07b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-12c635bd-4dab-5b49-9f10-53f8bdb5e07b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12c635bd-4dab-5b49-9f10-53f8bdb5e07b" :answer/id #uuid "12c635bd-4dab-5b49-9f10-53f8bdb5e07b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a135a2c5-e84d-52d5-89de-19fd76614f68" :question/id #uuid "a135a2c5-e84d-52d5-89de-19fd76614f68" :question/math-academy-id "q-90621" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-1=0$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the function $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ On the interval $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ the excluded values are $x=-\\pi ,\\,0.$\n\nOn the other hand, the function $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ On the interval $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ the excluded values are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-1 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x(2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1)-(2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1) & =0 \\\\ (2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1)(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1=0,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-2.$ But since $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|\\le 1,$ then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=2$ has no solution.\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}=-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-391ba5d5-1569-5749-804d-be4aff1f7cf2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-391ba5d5-1569-5749-804d-be4aff1f7cf2" :answer-field/id #uuid "391ba5d5-1569-5749-804d-be4aff1f7cf2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ed071515-aa7c-5061-b71d-58691f3581bd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ed071515-aa7c-5061-b71d-58691f3581bd" "answer-860130af-c136-524a-bff5-324d7b734e0c" "answer-8f90525e-3c3f-5e84-a145-5febbe233060" "answer-0a30a8d1-f851-5f5b-835f-f518b55fd72a" "answer-7ede5552-7e21-5d3d-aff7-357e5c363c62"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed071515-aa7c-5061-b71d-58691f3581bd" :answer/id #uuid "ed071515-aa7c-5061-b71d-58691f3581bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-860130af-c136-524a-bff5-324d7b734e0c" :answer/id #uuid "860130af-c136-524a-bff5-324d7b734e0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f90525e-3c3f-5e84-a145-5febbe233060" :answer/id #uuid "8f90525e-3c3f-5e84-a145-5febbe233060" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a30a8d1-f851-5f5b-835f-f518b55fd72a" :answer/id #uuid "0a30a8d1-f851-5f5b-835f-f518b55fd72a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ede5552-7e21-5d3d-aff7-357e5c363c62" :answer/id #uuid "7ede5552-7e21-5d3d-aff7-357e5c363c62" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-68f4987d-ed26-5738-89d9-828f518f37d4" :question/id #uuid "68f4987d-ed26-5738-89d9-828f518f37d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-338519" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1=0,$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Note that the functions $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ are not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0$ or $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ On the interval $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ the excluded values are $x=0,\\,\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pm \\pi .$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)-(2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1) & =0 \\\\ (2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1=0,\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-2.$ But since $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\le 1,$ then $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-2$ has no solution.\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ are $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nArranging all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a695e8c9-c3e6-555c-96b5-adf9863adba8" "answer-field-fa17f357-450d-5ebf-a036-6faafdfbae46"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a695e8c9-c3e6-555c-96b5-adf9863adba8" :answer-field/id #uuid "a695e8c9-c3e6-555c-96b5-adf9863adba8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-376ae09a-f4d9-52aa-9a15-740441b9501e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-376ae09a-f4d9-52aa-9a15-740441b9501e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-376ae09a-f4d9-52aa-9a15-740441b9501e" :answer/id #uuid "376ae09a-f4d9-52aa-9a15-740441b9501e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fa17f357-450d-5ebf-a036-6faafdfbae46" :answer-field/id #uuid "fa17f357-450d-5ebf-a036-6faafdfbae46" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ed0b24e5-bc5d-5d6d-81fb-99045cda4543" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ed0b24e5-bc5d-5d6d-81fb-99045cda4543"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed0b24e5-bc5d-5d6d-81fb-99045cda4543" :answer/id #uuid "ed0b24e5-bc5d-5d6d-81fb-99045cda4543" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fc1ed235-5329-5107-b309-feec267a8cde" :question/id #uuid "fc1ed235-5329-5107-b309-feec267a8cde" :question/math-academy-id "q-337369" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=0,$ for $0\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the functions $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ On the interval $0\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ the excluded values are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$ \n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=0.$ Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ so it is never equal to zero. Therefore, this equation has no solutions.\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently, $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={45}^{∘},\\,{225}^{∘}.$\n\nArranging all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={45}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={225}^{∘}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6bacd1dc-e01d-53e8-89a6-9fe722ce47a3" "answer-field-1f021667-0727-5fa1-9c53-57fb0ae2a54b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6bacd1dc-e01d-53e8-89a6-9fe722ce47a3" :answer-field/id #uuid "6bacd1dc-e01d-53e8-89a6-9fe722ce47a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e282431c-d64e-5706-b406-9225ec237285" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e282431c-d64e-5706-b406-9225ec237285"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e282431c-d64e-5706-b406-9225ec237285" :answer/id #uuid "e282431c-d64e-5706-b406-9225ec237285" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "45" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1f021667-0727-5fa1-9c53-57fb0ae2a54b" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f021667-0727-5fa1-9c53-57fb0ae2a54b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b8ac7c9a-5dac-540e-bb67-d19ed21fe95a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b8ac7c9a-5dac-540e-bb67-d19ed21fe95a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8ac7c9a-5dac-540e-bb67-d19ed21fe95a" :answer/id #uuid "b8ac7c9a-5dac-540e-bb67-d19ed21fe95a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "225" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cf8eafa9-ba3d-54d7-ae84-18acaa32c81c" :question/id #uuid "cf8eafa9-ba3d-54d7-ae84-18acaa32c81c" :question/math-academy-id "q-65411" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0$ for $0\\le x\\le \\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are written in ascending order, then what is ${x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the function $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ is not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ In the given interval, the excluded value is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x\\le \\pi $  are $x=0,\\,\\pi .$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2.$ The solution to this equation for $0\\le x\\le \\pi $ is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=0,\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,{x}_{3}=\\pi .\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\pi =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7de40b82-9df6-5bea-a14d-15c9e7e67103"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7de40b82-9df6-5bea-a14d-15c9e7e67103" :answer-field/id #uuid "7de40b82-9df6-5bea-a14d-15c9e7e67103" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-92843f39-23a0-5698-ac73-cdde16a2cae6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bfe443fa-730f-540d-a625-fe81728241b1" "answer-0aad9c1f-4c6f-563f-99c9-525eddb0b1fb" "answer-fe067070-e9db-5e0a-a3c7-2a529b04f2f9" "answer-92843f39-23a0-5698-ac73-cdde16a2cae6" "answer-419afc3f-fae1-5b04-9a9b-aa8c1df5d0f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfe443fa-730f-540d-a625-fe81728241b1" :answer/id #uuid "bfe443fa-730f-540d-a625-fe81728241b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0aad9c1f-4c6f-563f-99c9-525eddb0b1fb" :answer/id #uuid "0aad9c1f-4c6f-563f-99c9-525eddb0b1fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe067070-e9db-5e0a-a3c7-2a529b04f2f9" :answer/id #uuid "fe067070-e9db-5e0a-a3c7-2a529b04f2f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92843f39-23a0-5698-ac73-cdde16a2cae6" :answer/id #uuid "92843f39-23a0-5698-ac73-cdde16a2cae6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-419afc3f-fae1-5b04-9a9b-aa8c1df5d0f3" :answer/id #uuid "419afc3f-fae1-5b04-9a9b-aa8c1df5d0f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111147 :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the function $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ is not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ On the interval ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ the excluded values are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$ \n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=0,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=0.$ Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ so it is never equal to zero. Therefore, this equation has no solutions.\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$  are $x={30}^{∘},\\,{150}^{∘}.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={30}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={150}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=4\\,500.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186108617 :answer-field/correct "answer-241154b1-551f-584c-993e-0c47406592ed" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f8241f88-61bc-51f6-a6e0-1e5c1ffbf1a7" "answer-7a33462a-0b5c-5767-aee9-1552a464064f" "answer-241154b1-551f-584c-993e-0c47406592ed" "answer-9e2c5b50-3dd5-52c7-8399-36836c7f2379" "answer-de49b249-9f4d-5d00-80c8-43736c27b25e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8241f88-61bc-51f6-a6e0-1e5c1ffbf1a7" :answer/id #uuid "f8241f88-61bc-51f6-a6e0-1e5c1ffbf1a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a33462a-0b5c-5767-aee9-1552a464064f" :answer/id #uuid "7a33462a-0b5c-5767-aee9-1552a464064f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,250" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-241154b1-551f-584c-993e-0c47406592ed" :answer/id #uuid "241154b1-551f-584c-993e-0c47406592ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,500" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e2c5b50-3dd5-52c7-8399-36836c7f2379" :answer/id #uuid "9e2c5b50-3dd5-52c7-8399-36836c7f2379" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\,200" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de49b249-9f4d-5d00-80c8-43736c27b25e" :answer/id #uuid "de49b249-9f4d-5d00-80c8-43736c27b25e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-22a4714b-c9b9-595d-bd56-cdf122336677" :question/id #uuid "22a4714b-c9b9-595d-bd56-cdf122336677" :question/math-academy-id "q-82331" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1dd38a3d7e9969b9cc74c043261fd2f4d3ee1c1293a623fd812e31256b4ec766.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the curve $y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ on the interval ${0}^{∘}<x<{360}^{∘}.$ The curves intersect at the points $A,B,C$ and $D$, as shown. Find the product of the $x$-coordinates of $B$ and $D.$" :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$ values of both graphs are the same. This gives \nthe equation $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}<x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}<x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={210}^{∘},\\,{330}^{∘}.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={90}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={210}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}={270}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}={330}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the product of the $x$-coordinates of $B$ and $D$ is \n\n$$\n210\\cdot 330=69\\,300.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-82becc71-7589-5d67-812a-70d9948d739d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-82becc71-7589-5d67-812a-70d9948d739d" :answer-field/id #uuid "82becc71-7589-5d67-812a-70d9948d739d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a0773ce3-1a02-5702-aa2a-8171a60d2410" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f5af0e2c-ca25-50e1-858f-d52c9e9818f4" "answer-2d160033-4386-508d-b64f-d80f03cec15c" "answer-d4460c25-6796-5ccf-9fcf-f9e71d0d8b7f" "answer-a0773ce3-1a02-5702-aa2a-8171a60d2410" "answer-60466ba4-ad46-5796-b614-863d5b0d8100"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5af0e2c-ca25-50e1-858f-d52c9e9818f4" :answer/id #uuid "f5af0e2c-ca25-50e1-858f-d52c9e9818f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18\\,900" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d160033-4386-508d-b64f-d80f03cec15c" :answer/id #uuid "2d160033-4386-508d-b64f-d80f03cec15c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24\\,300" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4460c25-6796-5ccf-9fcf-f9e71d0d8b7f" :answer/id #uuid "d4460c25-6796-5ccf-9fcf-f9e71d0d8b7f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "29\\,700" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0773ce3-1a02-5702-aa2a-8171a60d2410" :answer/id #uuid "a0773ce3-1a02-5702-aa2a-8171a60d2410" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "69\\,300" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60466ba4-ad46-5796-b614-863d5b0d8100" :answer/id #uuid "60466ba4-ad46-5796-b614-863d5b0d8100" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "89\\,100" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-aa82973f-2912-57c5-acdd-b4b8b9abbe44" :question/id #uuid "aa82973f-2912-57c5-acdd-b4b8b9abbe44" :question/math-academy-id "q-338486" :question/problem "![](images/25/2593d513ce130090a84cc26e71bf321960712a6ccb2d8ff82e55e4728cc0657c.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=2{cos}^{2}⁡x$ and the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ on the interval $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ The curves intersect at the points $A,B,C,$ and $D,$ as shown. Determine the exact values of the $x$-coordinates of points $A$ and $D,$ in radians.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of point $A$ is\n{{field-1}}\nand the $x$-coordinate of point $D$ is\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$ values of both graphs are the same. This gives the equation $2{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1=0.\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$\n\nCombining the solutions and ordering them from smallest to largest, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,{x}_{3}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of point $A$ is\n$\\frac{\\pi }{3}$\nand the $x$-coordinate of point $D$ is\n$\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7726603e-9dba-5342-9622-38dad57d93fc" "answer-field-6de9e1be-49f8-52e7-a91c-12166b2a5eb3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7726603e-9dba-5342-9622-38dad57d93fc" :answer-field/id #uuid "7726603e-9dba-5342-9622-38dad57d93fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e2224658-7330-566b-9cbb-e995ea4833aa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e2224658-7330-566b-9cbb-e995ea4833aa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2224658-7330-566b-9cbb-e995ea4833aa" :answer/id #uuid "e2224658-7330-566b-9cbb-e995ea4833aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6de9e1be-49f8-52e7-a91c-12166b2a5eb3" :answer-field/id #uuid "6de9e1be-49f8-52e7-a91c-12166b2a5eb3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-99dce7ce-64f2-5c0a-9837-190cd80030e5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-99dce7ce-64f2-5c0a-9837-190cd80030e5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99dce7ce-64f2-5c0a-9837-190cd80030e5" :answer/id #uuid "99dce7ce-64f2-5c0a-9837-190cd80030e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111868 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\sin{x}\\cos{x}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin{x} = 0,  \\qquad 0^\\circ \\leq x \\leq 180^\\circ.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product rule, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\cos{x}-\\dfrac12=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sin{x}=0.$ The solutions to this equation that lie in the given domain are $x = 0^\\circ$ and $x=180^\\circ.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\cos{x}-\\dfrac12=0,$ or equivalently, $\\cos{x}=\\dfrac12.$ The only solution to this equation that lies in the given domain is $x = 60^\\circ.$\n\nCombining all the solutions from both equations, the solutions of the original equation (in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0^\\circ,\\quad x_2 = 60^\\circ,\\quad x_3 = 180^\\circ.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111869 :tutorial/content "We can solve some trigonometric equations containing reciprocal functions using the zero product rule.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n \n\n$$\n(\\sec x -1)(2\\sin x +1)= 0, \\qquad -\\pi \\leq x < \\pi.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the function $\\sec{x}$ is not defined at the points where $\\cos{x}=0.$ On the interval $[-\\pi,\\pi),$ the excluded values are $x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nThe equation is already factored, so by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\sec{x} -1 =0, \\qquad 2\\sin{x} + 1 = 0.\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sec{x} -1 =0,$ or equivalently, $\\sec{x} =1.$ The only solution to this equation for $-\\pi \\leq x < \\pi$ is $x = 0.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\sin{x} + 1 = 0,$ or equivalently, $\\sin{x} = -\\dfrac12.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\leq x < \\pi$ are $x=-\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$\n\nCombining all the solutions from both equations, the solutions to the original equation (in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 =  - \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},\\quad x_2 = - \\dfrac{\\pi}{6},\\quad x_3 = 0.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110840 :activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192798 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9473cd62-7434-5b05-96e0-e08528b86ef4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186193200 :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ The log-likelihood function of the sample $5.1,\\,4.2,\\,6.3$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +b\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =5.1+4.2+6.3=15.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=3.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\cdot 15.6 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(-15.6)\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=3+(-15.6)=-12.6.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186191086 :answer-field/correct 17592186191089 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-9473cd62-7434-5b05-96e0-e08528b86ef4" :question/id #uuid "9473cd62-7434-5b05-96e0-e08528b86ef4" :question/math-academy-id "q-159926" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ The log-likelihood function of the sample $8,\\,3,\\,5,\\,10$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +b\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =8+3+5+10=26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\cdot 26 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(-26)\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=4+(-26)=-22.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-afd91053-3ad7-5610-9011-77c03294ee47"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-afd91053-3ad7-5610-9011-77c03294ee47" :answer-field/id #uuid "afd91053-3ad7-5610-9011-77c03294ee47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-46dd295d-b967-55be-baa9-615ef8947546" :answer-field/choices ["answer-616179b8-6d31-545d-bd85-a8cbe1eea178" "answer-7880c273-b9f0-5ba3-92a1-bf42d3b1a839" "answer-46dd295d-b967-55be-baa9-615ef8947546" "answer-ef86d244-000d-57e4-bdb0-98870d062829" "answer-b20ab84a-f38e-5407-8c0a-21d8ce7f5813"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-616179b8-6d31-545d-bd85-a8cbe1eea178" :answer/id #uuid "616179b8-6d31-545d-bd85-a8cbe1eea178" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7880c273-b9f0-5ba3-92a1-bf42d3b1a839" :answer/id #uuid "7880c273-b9f0-5ba3-92a1-bf42d3b1a839" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46dd295d-b967-55be-baa9-615ef8947546" :answer/id #uuid "46dd295d-b967-55be-baa9-615ef8947546" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-22" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef86d244-000d-57e4-bdb0-98870d062829" :answer/id #uuid "ef86d244-000d-57e4-bdb0-98870d062829" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b20ab84a-f38e-5407-8c0a-21d8ce7f5813" :answer/id #uuid "b20ab84a-f38e-5407-8c0a-21d8ce7f5813" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192686 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-47a49d65-e85d-5b9b-a8c6-e21bc37526ef" "question-5c4c55fe-2946-5795-a8c6-76c349270710" "question-aaf34c24-70f2-59f0-ad3e-3eb728abaa5d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-47a49d65-e85d-5b9b-a8c6-e21bc37526ef" :question/id #uuid "47a49d65-e85d-5b9b-a8c6-e21bc37526ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-159947" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,{1}^{2}),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{10},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}=41.2,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}^{2}=182.6.\n$$\n\n \nGiven that the log-likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+a+b\\theta +c{\\theta }^{2},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $\\theta $ and standard deviation $1$ is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=10$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}=41.2,$ ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}^{2}=182.6,$ and $n=10.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{10}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(182.6-2\\theta \\cdot 41.2+10{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+(-91.3)+41.2\\theta +(-5){\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=(-91.3)+41.2+(-5)=-55.1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-689ba3cb-114a-56ff-8abb-81f20075f5f3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-689ba3cb-114a-56ff-8abb-81f20075f5f3" :answer-field/id #uuid "689ba3cb-114a-56ff-8abb-81f20075f5f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-48337ab4-cd65-553e-b93c-57c20fa8194e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ebf9a326-b0aa-5098-aa51-d8aa62a227d1" "answer-48337ab4-cd65-553e-b93c-57c20fa8194e" "answer-42f17b6e-adee-5418-8a80-24659c92c3a5" "answer-2dbd9de5-ffb6-5a92-af24-1a190f516d4b" "answer-a31bea97-b4ca-5c19-a543-03494bd16286"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ebf9a326-b0aa-5098-aa51-d8aa62a227d1" :answer/id #uuid "ebf9a326-b0aa-5098-aa51-d8aa62a227d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-51.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48337ab4-cd65-553e-b93c-57c20fa8194e" :answer/id #uuid "48337ab4-cd65-553e-b93c-57c20fa8194e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-55.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42f17b6e-adee-5418-8a80-24659c92c3a5" :answer/id #uuid "42f17b6e-adee-5418-8a80-24659c92c3a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-57.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2dbd9de5-ffb6-5a92-af24-1a190f516d4b" :answer/id #uuid "2dbd9de5-ffb6-5a92-af24-1a190f516d4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "52.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a31bea97-b4ca-5c19-a543-03494bd16286" :answer/id #uuid "a31bea97-b4ca-5c19-a543-03494bd16286" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "53.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5c4c55fe-2946-5795-a8c6-76c349270710" :question/id #uuid "5c4c55fe-2946-5795-a8c6-76c349270710" :question/math-academy-id "q-159960" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,{1}^{2}),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. The log-likelihood function of the sample $0.5,\\,-0.8,\\,-1.3$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-1.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+a+b\\theta +c{\\theta }^{2}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma })}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0.5+(-0.8)+(-1.3) \\\\  & =-1.6 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.5)^{2}+(-0.8)^{2}+(-1.3)^{2} \\\\  & =2.58\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=3.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(2.58-2\\theta \\cdot (-1.6)+3{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-1.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+(-1.29)+(-1.6)\\theta +(-1.5){\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=-1.29+(-1.6)+(-1.5)=-4.39.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-32c3ae4e-5559-5bc7-8d50-9bcf40432395"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-32c3ae4e-5559-5bc7-8d50-9bcf40432395" :answer-field/id #uuid "32c3ae4e-5559-5bc7-8d50-9bcf40432395" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e5189590-8a2a-5040-af84-68d2107b8f6b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5f7bf1e4-9398-55b3-bd2b-8515162867e4" "answer-e5189590-8a2a-5040-af84-68d2107b8f6b" "answer-f57a279b-4104-5ca5-8808-72ea4b362832" "answer-94e0befa-4705-51e9-9630-de948e992209" "answer-a8ba97b2-62e5-5ad9-99a5-29d92dd18008"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f7bf1e4-9398-55b3-bd2b-8515162867e4" :answer/id #uuid "5f7bf1e4-9398-55b3-bd2b-8515162867e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4.14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5189590-8a2a-5040-af84-68d2107b8f6b" :answer/id #uuid "e5189590-8a2a-5040-af84-68d2107b8f6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4.39" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f57a279b-4104-5ca5-8808-72ea4b362832" :answer/id #uuid "f57a279b-4104-5ca5-8808-72ea4b362832" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4.99" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94e0befa-4705-51e9-9630-de948e992209" :answer/id #uuid "94e0befa-4705-51e9-9630-de948e992209" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4.18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8ba97b2-62e5-5ad9-99a5-29d92dd18008" :answer/id #uuid "a8ba97b2-62e5-5ad9-99a5-29d92dd18008" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4.54" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-aaf34c24-70f2-59f0-ad3e-3eb728abaa5d" :question/id #uuid "aaf34c24-70f2-59f0-ad3e-3eb728abaa5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-159961" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4},{X}_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,{1}^{2}),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. The log-likelihood function of the sample $0.7,\\,0.6,\\,-0.5,\\,1.7,\\,-1.1$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-2.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+a+b\\theta +c{\\theta }^{2}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma })}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0.7+0.6+(-0.5)+1.7+(-1.1) \\\\  & =1.4 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.7)^{2}+(0.6)^{2}+(-0.5)^{2}+(1.7)^{2}+(-1.1)^{2} \\\\  & =5.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=5.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(5.2-2\\theta \\cdot (1.4)+5{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-2.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+(-2.6)+(1.4)\\theta +(-2.5){\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=-2.6+(1.4)+(-2.5)=-3.7.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2253fa4d-3bff-5c5d-817e-be26b444097e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2253fa4d-3bff-5c5d-817e-be26b444097e" :answer-field/id #uuid "2253fa4d-3bff-5c5d-817e-be26b444097e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7eef2b9f-e7ca-51a3-b197-a936d17dd502" :answer-field/choices ["answer-47698303-8d61-5840-8827-86edae5c0ca9" "answer-cc28b179-5541-543f-9559-8ecc8381e121" "answer-7eef2b9f-e7ca-51a3-b197-a936d17dd502" "answer-48989a49-93e8-5124-b600-ec90d999d72c" "answer-e5647be5-c113-5d6c-87be-376b749ccd3e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47698303-8d61-5840-8827-86edae5c0ca9" :answer/id #uuid "47698303-8d61-5840-8827-86edae5c0ca9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc28b179-5541-543f-9559-8ecc8381e121" :answer/id #uuid "cc28b179-5541-543f-9559-8ecc8381e121" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7eef2b9f-e7ca-51a3-b197-a936d17dd502" :answer/id #uuid "7eef2b9f-e7ca-51a3-b197-a936d17dd502" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3.7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48989a49-93e8-5124-b600-ec90d999d72c" :answer/id #uuid "48989a49-93e8-5124-b600-ec90d999d72c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5647be5-c113-5d6c-87be-376b749ccd3e" :answer/id #uuid "e5647be5-c113-5d6c-87be-376b749ccd3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-e3eeaafd-261d-5d3a-b382-2db7d6832fa1" :activity/id #uuid "e3eeaafd-261d-5d3a-b382-2db7d6832fa1" :activity/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Continuous Probability Distributions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049417 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-b8626d06-e8cc-5e08-8191-4195c906b582" "step-8181d357-c830-5d4e-876f-952449aa0c43" "step-19ee89b5-e5c0-5578-ad68-47b6d34875da" "step-2d2f87b7-e858-5638-87e1-255173ffd6fc" "step-6431c930-d07c-5012-83c3-a683117b63b6"] :activity/first-step "step-b8626d06-e8cc-5e08-8191-4195c906b582" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-b8626d06-e8cc-5e08-8191-4195c906b582" :step/id #uuid "b8626d06-e8cc-5e08-8191-4195c906b582" :step/content 17592186193200 :step/next "step-8181d357-c830-5d4e-876f-952449aa0c43" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8181d357-c830-5d4e-876f-952449aa0c43" :step/id #uuid "8181d357-c830-5d4e-876f-952449aa0c43" :step/content "question-9473cd62-7434-5b05-96e0-e08528b86ef4" :step/next "step-19ee89b5-e5c0-5578-ad68-47b6d34875da" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-19ee89b5-e5c0-5578-ad68-47b6d34875da" :step/id #uuid "19ee89b5-e5c0-5578-ad68-47b6d34875da" :step/content "question-47a49d65-e85d-5b9b-a8c6-e21bc37526ef" :step/next "step-2d2f87b7-e858-5638-87e1-255173ffd6fc" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2d2f87b7-e858-5638-87e1-255173ffd6fc" :step/id #uuid "2d2f87b7-e858-5638-87e1-255173ffd6fc" :step/content "question-5c4c55fe-2946-5795-a8c6-76c349270710" :step/next "step-6431c930-d07c-5012-83c3-a683117b63b6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6431c930-d07c-5012-83c3-a683117b63b6" :step/id #uuid "6431c930-d07c-5012-83c3-a683117b63b6" :step/content "question-aaf34c24-70f2-59f0-ad3e-3eb728abaa5d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078546 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d9c0d129-478a-533e-ada4-db9e83c26531" "question-e6d790e0-5c33-5dec-949d-78146efef13a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d9c0d129-478a-533e-ada4-db9e83c26531" :question/id #uuid "d9c0d129-478a-533e-ada4-db9e83c26531" :question/math-academy-id "q-67450" :question/problem "Given that $8$, $2x$, and $x$ are the first three terms of a geometric sequence, find $x.$" :question/worked-solution "The first three terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=8,\\,{a}_{2}=2x,\\,{a}_{3}=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio of the first two terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{2x}{8}=\\frac{x}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the common ratio of the second and third terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{x}{2x}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the values for $r$ and solving for $x,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cbcf9c63-b025-5bf8-b0d4-e2ed9ac04b61"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cbcf9c63-b025-5bf8-b0d4-e2ed9ac04b61" :answer-field/id #uuid "cbcf9c63-b025-5bf8-b0d4-e2ed9ac04b61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1f143723-8c41-5def-9acf-e95d5fa37bbb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4d9ca40a-7358-522b-995f-fa037a087917" "answer-1f143723-8c41-5def-9acf-e95d5fa37bbb" "answer-89381255-0dc6-50c2-a04d-d0c5657a7967" "answer-28ecaefe-65a4-5f0b-9144-0855a5975c58" "answer-7aed2b30-e60d-54fa-8d06-59669d0d72ab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d9ca40a-7358-522b-995f-fa037a087917" :answer/id #uuid "4d9ca40a-7358-522b-995f-fa037a087917" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f143723-8c41-5def-9acf-e95d5fa37bbb" :answer/id #uuid "1f143723-8c41-5def-9acf-e95d5fa37bbb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89381255-0dc6-50c2-a04d-d0c5657a7967" :answer/id #uuid "89381255-0dc6-50c2-a04d-d0c5657a7967" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28ecaefe-65a4-5f0b-9144-0855a5975c58" :answer/id #uuid "28ecaefe-65a4-5f0b-9144-0855a5975c58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7aed2b30-e60d-54fa-8d06-59669d0d72ab" :answer/id #uuid "7aed2b30-e60d-54fa-8d06-59669d0d72ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078547 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-28e7f38a-8ff9-507f-8c27-7cd729b83484" "question-b85423f1-a316-59dd-932f-ac0693226903"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-28e7f38a-8ff9-507f-8c27-7cd729b83484" :question/id #uuid "28e7f38a-8ff9-507f-8c27-7cd729b83484" :question/math-academy-id "q-79508" :question/problem "Which of the sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $-3,\\,33,\\,-363,\\,…$\n\nII. $4,\\,52,\\,572,\\,…$\n\nIII. $-2,\\,24,\\,-312,\\,…$" :question/worked-solution "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{33}{(-3)}=-11 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-363)}{33}=-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{52}{4}=13 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{572}{52}=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{24}{(-2)}=-12 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-312)}{24}=-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequence I could be a geometric sequence." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c39a56b0-17ca-5ba8-97f8-8c9ae43975ba"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c39a56b0-17ca-5ba8-97f8-8c9ae43975ba" :answer-field/id #uuid "c39a56b0-17ca-5ba8-97f8-8c9ae43975ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-33cbb00f-07b5-51f0-be9d-4a6856c47b23" :answer-field/choices ["answer-995472d2-4a77-572a-a7b7-711efd1e590b" "answer-33cbb00f-07b5-51f0-be9d-4a6856c47b23" "answer-dd60e458-a551-5764-8eed-77a2db9e815e" "answer-d8403a05-8be0-5213-a984-3ab18c368b94" "answer-560c99d8-6dc4-559c-9c1c-50a616a7568f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-995472d2-4a77-572a-a7b7-711efd1e590b" :answer/id #uuid "995472d2-4a77-572a-a7b7-711efd1e590b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33cbb00f-07b5-51f0-be9d-4a6856c47b23" :answer/id #uuid "33cbb00f-07b5-51f0-be9d-4a6856c47b23" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd60e458-a551-5764-8eed-77a2db9e815e" :answer/id #uuid "dd60e458-a551-5764-8eed-77a2db9e815e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8403a05-8be0-5213-a984-3ab18c368b94" :answer/id #uuid "d8403a05-8be0-5213-a984-3ab18c368b94" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-560c99d8-6dc4-559c-9c1c-50a616a7568f" :answer/id #uuid "560c99d8-6dc4-559c-9c1c-50a616a7568f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b85423f1-a316-59dd-932f-ac0693226903" :question/id #uuid "b85423f1-a316-59dd-932f-ac0693226903" :question/math-academy-id "q-109800" :question/problem "Which of the sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $2,\\,4,\\,10,\\,…$\n\nII. $-5,\\,10,\\,-20,\\,…$\n\nIII. $1,\\,-4,\\,-16,\\,…$" :question/worked-solution "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{4}{2}=2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{10}{4}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{10}{(-5)}=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-20)}{10}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-4)}{1}=-4 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-16)}{(-4)}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequence II could be a geometric sequence." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f0da2e57-6d30-583d-ba2b-acd1651fbc57"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f0da2e57-6d30-583d-ba2b-acd1651fbc57" :answer-field/id #uuid "f0da2e57-6d30-583d-ba2b-acd1651fbc57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-37e1d3e6-abcb-56cf-b2d6-40a027a22288" :answer-field/choices ["answer-aae1215c-ff4b-50d9-8fea-cc3646342824" "answer-d1fb87dd-e1e7-56d0-aea8-fb448cd18b13" "answer-c95d0dda-8d34-5570-b249-1ee8b36c4fbe" "answer-37e1d3e6-abcb-56cf-b2d6-40a027a22288" "answer-567f9117-9e54-5558-81ff-a15554a23e2e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aae1215c-ff4b-50d9-8fea-cc3646342824" :answer/id #uuid "aae1215c-ff4b-50d9-8fea-cc3646342824" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1fb87dd-e1e7-56d0-aea8-fb448cd18b13" :answer/id #uuid "d1fb87dd-e1e7-56d0-aea8-fb448cd18b13" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c95d0dda-8d34-5570-b249-1ee8b36c4fbe" :answer/id #uuid "c95d0dda-8d34-5570-b249-1ee8b36c4fbe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II  and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37e1d3e6-abcb-56cf-b2d6-40a027a22288" :answer/id #uuid "37e1d3e6-abcb-56cf-b2d6-40a027a22288" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-567f9117-9e54-5558-81ff-a15554a23e2e" :answer/id #uuid "567f9117-9e54-5558-81ff-a15554a23e2e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078545 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-abfcac51-46f8-5836-b296-e3921e8850cc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-abfcac51-46f8-5836-b296-e3921e8850cc" :question/id #uuid "abfcac51-46f8-5836-b296-e3921e8850cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-250665" :question/problem "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n-4,\\,16,\\,-64,\\,256,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe fifth term of this sequence is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "![](images/84/84c714b0c37c7f28cb83c59e3455755470ff6aa69da145c80ccea19200b1ad8f.png)\n\nNotice that the sequence is geometric, with a common ratio of \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{16}{(-4)}=-4.\n$$\n\nSo, we can get each term by multiplying the previous term by $-4.$ \n\nContinuing to list terms of the sequence until we get to the $5$th term, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/40/400ef822bd63631f65a4190f8367e84878c03a302c3202a112e9de2858ae4249.png)\n\nTherefore, the $5$th term is $-1\\,024.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-37ff7102-c223-52be-869b-375cd2da2fab"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-37ff7102-c223-52be-869b-375cd2da2fab" :answer-field/id #uuid "37ff7102-c223-52be-869b-375cd2da2fab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-27ff576a-8b4f-5191-bef3-da36f3c85be2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-27ff576a-8b4f-5191-bef3-da36f3c85be2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27ff576a-8b4f-5191-bef3-da36f3c85be2" :answer/id #uuid "27ff576a-8b4f-5191-bef3-da36f3c85be2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1024" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e6d790e0-5c33-5dec-949d-78146efef13a" :question/id #uuid "e6d790e0-5c33-5dec-949d-78146efef13a" :question/math-academy-id "q-250840" :question/problem "Given that $\\frac{x}{4}$, $x$, and $64$ are the first three terms of a geometric sequence, find $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The first three terms are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=\\frac{x}{4},\\,{a}_{2}=x,\\,{a}_{3}=64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio of the first two terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{x}{(\\frac{x}{4})}=x\\cdot \\frac{4}{x}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the common ratio of the second and third terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{64}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the values for $r$ and solving for $x,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =\\frac{64}{x} \\\\ 4x & =64 \\\\ x & =\\frac{64}{4} \\\\ x & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a12b2c7e-438f-5baa-bede-06e74205a643"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a12b2c7e-438f-5baa-bede-06e74205a643" :answer-field/id #uuid "a12b2c7e-438f-5baa-bede-06e74205a643" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c75f4c4b-c7a0-59c5-8797-cff98d56b03c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c75f4c4b-c7a0-59c5-8797-cff98d56b03c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c75f4c4b-c7a0-59c5-8797-cff98d56b03c" :answer/id #uuid "c75f4c4b-c7a0-59c5-8797-cff98d56b03c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-40989a66-8646-5f19-90b9-e0ec4776e375" :activity/id #uuid "40989a66-8646-5f19-90b9-e0ec4776e375" :activity/title "Introduction to Geometric Sequences" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050977 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-8bf393cb-72f3-500f-a512-82e3760658ff" "step-0218cccb-f086-56d9-b8f9-aa20f5c1b9cb" "step-59f07007-7ea1-58c4-9a83-d350969d589e" "step-329da224-1a13-5b74-9041-4ca85